[[entries]]
name = "per_os_target_flags_win_vs_linux"
description = "v5.5.0 introduced CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN vs CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX preprocessor flags for per-OS stdlib selection"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.5.0 ships `cycc_win` (the Windows cross-compiler) alongside
`cycc` and `cycc_aarch64`. The three cross-entries now predefine
a target-OS flag so stdlib selectors can include the right peer:

    cycc            predefines  CYRIUS_ARCH_X86     + CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX
    cycc_aarch64    predefines  CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64 + CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX
    cycc_win        predefines  CYRIUS_ARCH_X86     + CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN

The two target-OS flags are mutually exclusive. Selectors use
paired `#ifdef` guards rather than `#ifdef` + `#else` (cyrius's
preprocessor has no `#else` / `#ifndef` yet):

    #ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN
    include "lib/syscalls_windows.cyr"
    #endif
    #ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX
    #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86
    include "lib/syscalls_x86_64_linux.cyr"
    #endif
    #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64
    include "lib/syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr"
    #endif
    #endif

Only one of the three inner blocks runs per compile. Adding a
new target OS (e.g. BSD) is another predefined flag + another
top-level block in the selector.

When writing stdlib modules that have OS-specific behaviour, use
the same pattern:
    lib/foo.cyr          public dispatch (selector)
    lib/foo_linux.cyr    Linux peer
    lib/foo_windows.cyr  Windows peer

[[entries]]
name = "struct_return_by_value_5_5_36"
description = "`fn make(): Point { ... return p; }` compiles — struct return is native on both SysV and Win64"
category = "cyrius"
---
Pre-v5.5.36 callers had to pass `&Point` out-params; the
compiler didn't wire struct-by-value return for >8B types.
v5.5.36 closed it:

    fn make_point(x: int, y: int): Point {
        stack var p: Point;
        p.x = x;
        p.y = y;
        return p;
    }

    fn caller() {
        var got: Point = make_point(3, 4);  // deep copy
    }

ABI plumbing (automatic):
 - SysV: hidden retptr in RDI (first arg).
 - Win64: hidden retptr in RCX (first arg).
 - Callee memcpys the local into *retptr before RET.

The `var got: Point = make_point(...)` form on the LHS is also
new in v5.5.36 — parser recognises struct-returning call on the
RHS and emits the retptr setup.

Use return-by-value for small packed values (Point, Range,
Result). Long-lived structs should still live on the heap.

[[entries]]
name = "variadic_fn_5_5_36"
description = "`fn printf(fmt: str, ...)` syntax lands in v5.5.36 (Phase 3-min); use lib/varargs.cyr inside the body"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.5.36 added `...` as the variadic-parameter token:

    fn printf(fmt: str, ...) {
        // use lib/varargs.cyr va_start / va_arg / va_end
    }

`...` is TOK=112, only valid as the last parameter. The compiler
sets a GFVA flag on the callee so call sites know to handle
variadic ABI quirks:
 - Win64 MSDN rule: float args to a variadic callee go into
   BOTH the XMM register file AND the RCX/RDX/R8/R9 GPR slots.
   The compiler emits the duplication at the call site; you
   don't write it.
 - SysV passes float args in XMM0-7 as usual; RAX on the call
   holds the number of XMM regs used, per SysV §3.5.7.

Phase 3-min (shipped v5.5.36) covers primitives-through-varargs.
Phase 3-full (future) expands va_arg to structs-by-value and
nested varargs — for now, if you need those shapes, pass a
pointer or a single `u64` and unpack.

[[entries]]
name = "fncall6_ceiling_into_extern_c"
description = "Cyrius fns that internally fncall* into wgpu-native (or any extern-C) were reported to segfault at 7+ params; pure Cyrius has NO arity limit (5-20 verified v6.4.64). UNVERIFIED since v6.4.64, which fixed a Win64 stack-arg codegen P0 in the same shape — premise-check before adopting the struct-pointer workaround."
category = "cyrius"
---
**PREMISE-CHECK BEFORE ADOPTING THE WORKAROUND (added at the
v6.4.82 closeout).** This entry has not been re-verified since
**v6.4.64**, which fixed a real Win64 codegen P0 in exactly this
shape: `ECALLPOPS`'s PE branch shuttled stack args through a fixed
5-register table with no code path past `nextra == 5`, so a call at
10+ args both mis-popped the register args and emitted NO stack-arg
writes — silently corrupting argument 1. SysV had had the same class
fixed at v5.6.24 / v6.0.57. It is entirely possible that some or all
of the crash described below was that compiler bug rather than an
inherent FFI boundary. Reproduce the 7-arg crash on your current
toolchain before restructuring your API around it — and if it
reproduces, that is a compiler bug to FILE, not a rule to code
around. (This is the language repo: when the compiler can't compile
valid cyrius, fix the compiler.)

---

A Cyrius fn signature CAN take 7+ args without issue when the
body is pure Cyrius — **there is no cyrius arity limit** (5-20 args
verified on real Windows / Linux / aarch64 at v6.4.64). The claim
below is specifically about the extern-C boundary: the moment the
body contains a `fncall*`
into wgpu-native (or any extern-C function table), the whole
signature must stay at ≤ 6 args or the call segfaults at
runtime.

Example shape that crashes:

```cyrius
fn _backend_wgpu_some_op(ctx, a, b, c, d, e, f) {  # 7 args
    fncall6(slot, a, b, c, d, e, f);                # crash
}
```

Same fn at 6 args works. Same fn at 7 args without the inner
fncall* works (pure Cyrius all the way down).

**Why.** The Cyrius calling convention for fns that internally
make C-ABI calls passes args through registers in a way that
doesn't fully line up with the System V x86_64 ABI when the
caller has 7+ args of its own. The first 6 params land in
rdi/rsi/rdx/rcx/r8/r9 cleanly; the 7th would go on the stack,
and the stack alignment / spill protocol differs from how
wgpu-native expects to be called. Result: register clobber that
manifests as a segfault inside the wgpu fn, not at the call
site.

**The fix.** Pack 7+ args into a struct pointer:

```cyrius
fn _backend_wgpu_some_op(ctx, args_ptr) {
    var a = load64(args_ptr + 0);
    var b = load64(args_ptr + 8);
    # ... unpack rest ...
    fncall6(slot, a, b, c, d, e, f);
}
```

Caller side allocates a struct, fills the fields, passes the
ptr. This is the pattern mabda uses for `wgpu_*` ops with 7+
inputs (e.g., `wgpu_command_encoder_copy_buffer_to_buffer`).

**When it doesn't apply.** Pure Cyrius fns with no inner
fncall* into extern-C — those take 12+ args fine. mabda's
internal helpers like `native_pm4_build_render_clear_triangle`
take 6 args and would work at 12 if needed.

**Cross-pollination.** Same constraint will apply to any
Cyrius project that builds a function-table-of-extern-C-fns
abstraction: `samvada` (sd_bus C shim), future
`vidya_logind`-style packages, anything wrapping libc beyond
the stdlib. Document at the boundary; don't trial-and-error.

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_callee_saved_and_imm_limits"
description = "AArch64 cross-`bl` clobbers x0–x18 (caller-saved); `cmp xN, #imm` is 12-bit unsigned, `mov xN, #imm` is 16-bit — larger values need ldr literal-pool"
category = "cyrius"
---
THREE CONJOINED AArch64 ABI / encoding gotchas, all surfaced
during the v2.3.3 → v2.3.4 vidya backfill. Different in
mechanism, identical in symptom: the assembler accepts the
source, the binary runs, the output is wrong.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
1. Caller-saved vs callee-saved across `bl`
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

AArch64 AAPCS64 reserves 32 GP registers split into roles. The
ones that matter for hand-written asm calling helper fns:

    x0–x7    — argument / return value (caller-saved)
    x8       — indirect result location (caller-saved)
    x9–x15   — temporaries (caller-saved)
    x16, x17 — intra-procedure-call scratch (caller-saved)
    x18      — platform register / TLS scratch (caller-saved)
    x19–x28  — callee-saved (helper preserves on entry, restores on exit)
    x29      — frame pointer
    x30      — link register (return address)

Any value held in x0–x18 across a `bl` to a helper is **assumed
gone** unless the caller spilled it. The helper is free to
clobber every one of those registers — and stdlib helpers
DO clobber them, even when their visible job is "compute one
small thing."

Two rescue patterns:

  (a) Cache loop state in callee-saved registers x19–x28:

      stp x29, x30, [sp, #-16]!
      stp x19, x20, [sp, #-16]!     # save callee-saveds before use
      mov x19, x0                    # cache loop counter
      mov x20, x1                    # cache base ptr
    .L1:
      mov x0, x19                    # restore from cache
      bl  helper                     # x0–x18 clobbered, x19/x20 fine
      add x19, x19, #1
      cmp x19, #100
      b.lt .L1
      ldp x19, x20, [sp], #16
      ldp x29, x30, [sp], #16
      ret

      The `stp/ldp` pair before/after the loop is mandatory —
      the helper expects to find x19/x20 unchanged when *it*
      returns to ITS caller, which is your function returning
      to ITS caller. Forgetting the spill turns a clean local
      bug into a callee-clobber that surfaces several frames
      up.

  (b) Recompute after each call, don't cache:

      mov x0, x9                     # x9 holds idx
      bl  helper                     # x9 may be clobbered now
      ldr x9, [sp, #16]              # reload idx from frame
      add x9, x9, #1
      str x9, [sp, #16]
      ...

      Slower (one ldr/str per iteration vs free register reads)
      but doesn't require setting up a callee-saved spill at
      function entry. Use when the loop is shallow or the
      cached value is only read once per call.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

  v2.3.3  grid_pathfinding/asm_x86_64.s — A* helper called
          `manhattan` via bl; the outer loop's row/col indices
          lived in caller-saved regs and got clobbered. Same
          symptom on AArch64 sibling. Caught by the BFS-vs-A*
          path-length agreement test.
  v2.3.3  maze_generation/asm_x86_64.s — `idx` register
          clobbered across the PCG `rng_next` bl. PCG output
          sequence diverged from the reference seed=42 trace.
  v2.3.4  btree_indexing/asm_aarch64.s — node-walk loop cached
          parent pointer in x10 across `bl key_cmp`. Tree
          walk corrupted on first split. 3rd surfacing.
  v2.3.4  sql_parsing/asm_aarch64.s — token-stream loop cached
          token index in x12. SELECT validator silently
          accepted malformed input. 4th surfacing.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
2. `cmp xN, #imm` is 12-bit unsigned
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

The immediate field of `cmp xN, #imm` (and `subs xzr, xN, #imm`,
which is what `cmp` is) is 12 bits unsigned, with an optional
LSL #12 shift. So the legal range without shift is 0–4095, and
with the shift it's 4096–16777215 in steps of 4096 — useful for
page boundaries, useless for arbitrary numbers.

    cmp x0, #4095          # OK
    cmp x0, #4096          # ERROR: immediate out of range
    cmp x0, #4166          # ERROR (DT_US/4 = 4166)

CORRECT SHAPE — load the immediate into a temp register, then
compare register-to-register:

    ldr x16, =4166         # literal pool (assembler emits an
                           # ldr from a PC-relative pool slot)
    cmp x0, x16            # OK — register form has no immediate

`x16` (IP0) is the canonical scratch register for this — it's
caller-saved AND reserved for intra-procedure-call use, so the
linker won't repurpose it for veneer insertion.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

  v2.3.3  game_loop_architecture/asm_aarch64.s — fixed-timestep
          accumulator compared against `DT_US/4 = 4166`. The
          assembler error was clear ("immediate out of range")
          but the fix wasn't obvious — the natural reflex is
          "use shift," which doesn't apply here.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
3. `mov xN, #imm` is 16-bit
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

`mov xN, #imm` is a synthetic instruction that the assembler
expands to `movz` (move with zero) or `movn` (move with NOT).
Both encodings carry a 16-bit immediate plus a 2-bit shift
selector (LSL by 0 / 16 / 32 / 48). So a single `mov` covers
any 16-bit value, plus 16-bit values shifted to any quarter of
the 64-bit word.

For a fully arbitrary 64-bit constant, the assembler chains
`movz` + up to three `movk` (move with keep) instructions,
each filling one 16-bit slot. Most assemblers do this expansion
silently when you write `mov xN, #imm` — but the failure mode
when the value can't be expressed cleanly is, again, "immediate
out of range."

    mov x0, #65535         # OK — single movz
    mov x0, #65536         # OK — single movz, LSL #16
    mov x0, #100000        # error or silent-wrong (depends on as)
    mov x0, #1442695040888963407   # error — needs 4-instr chain

CORRECT SHAPE — same rescue as `cmp`: literal pool + ldr.

    ldr x0, =1442695040888963407   # PCG64 default-stream
                                   # increment; one instruction,
                                   # one 8-byte pool slot

The assembler chooses between mov-chain (no pool, 4 instructions,
no memory access) and ldr-from-pool (1 instruction, 1 memory
load) based on the constant's shape. For PCG-style 64-bit
multipliers and ChaCha-style state words, ldr-from-pool always
wins.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

  v2.3.3  maze_generation/asm_aarch64.s — PCG multiplier
          0x5851F42D4C957F2D loaded via mov, only the bottom
          16 bits actually landed. Maze generation produced
          deterministic output, but the wrong deterministic
          output. Caught by the cross-language byte-parity
          assertion (`cells[0]=13, cells[27]=12, cells[63]=6`)
          when the AArch64 port disagreed with all 9 other
          ports.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
The pattern across all three
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

AArch64's encoding-tight ISA + the AAPCS64 split means writing
asm for it differs from x86_64 in three predictable ways:

  - You can't dump arbitrary 64-bit constants into instructions.
    Use the literal pool (`ldr xN, =value`) by reflex when the
    constant exceeds 16 bits.
  - You can't park values in caller-saved regs across a `bl`.
    Decide once per function: callee-saved (set up a spill at
    entry, pay one stp + one ldp) OR memory (one ldr/str per
    use, slower but no setup).
  - The assembler error messages are mostly accurate, but they
    don't suggest the literal-pool fix. The reflex is to add a
    shift; the literal pool is the right answer ~90% of the
    time.

x86_64 hides all three: arbitrary 64-bit immediates fit in
`movabs`, caller-saved registers are fewer (rax, rcx, rdx, rsi,
rdi, r8–r11), so the surface area of "what can I keep across a
call" is smaller and more obvious. Porting an x86_64 asm
example to aarch64 always surfaces at least one of these three.

[[entries]]
name = "agnos_two_sided_abi_mirror"
description = "An `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` syscall-wrapper band is byte-identical on every non-agnos target; verify agnos-only wrappers via agnos-crossbuild-gate.sh + a call-probe. The a4=r10 5-arg syscall form rides r10 (push→pop)."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.4.2** — the agnos audio syscall band (`sys_snd_*`, #64–#69) landed as the ring-3 half of a two-sided ABI (kernel HDA handlers land in parallel on the agnos arm). The pattern for adding an agnos syscall wrapper, and why it's low-risk to the cross-OS self-host:

THE TWO-SIDED MIRROR. `lib/syscalls_x86_64_agnos.cyr` is a STANDALONE faithful mirror of `agnos/docs/development/agnos-userland-abi.md` — it does NOT include `syscalls_linux_common.cyr`. agnos has its own numbers, exit=0 (not Linux 60), AO_* open flags (not Linux O_*), and the explicit-length invariant (every path arg carries its length — no NUL assumption). The register convention IS the same as x86_64-Linux (rax=num, rdi/rsi/rdx/r10 = a1..a4), so the `syscall` instruction EMIT is unchanged — only the NUMBERS differ. RE-FREEZE RULE (§5): the kernel doc is canonical; this file mirrors it. If a number/signature changes in the doc, re-freeze here.

BYTE-IDENTICAL EVERYWHERE ELSE. The wrappers are `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS`-gated (the flag is predefined only for the agnos emit target, `CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS=1`; `src/main.cyr` reads it early). So they're #ifdef'd OUT of every non-agnos build → cycc self-hosts byte-identical on Linux/macOS/Windows/aarch64, and seed → cybs → cycc stays byte-identical. A pure-additive agnos wrapper band is NOT a compiler change.

HOW TO VERIFY AGNOS-ONLY CODE (a Linux-hosted cycc can't run it). Two gates, both host-side: (1) the standing `scripts/agnos-crossbuild-gate.sh` (9 sub-checks → a valid agnos ELF) must stay green; (2) a CALL-PROBE — a tiny program that calls every new wrapper, compiled with `build/cyrius build --agnos`, must produce a valid agnos ELF (rc=0, no reachable-undefined fn) and emit `syscall` with the right `eax` numbers. Runtime (HDA → ALC897 for audio) validates QEMU-side on the agnos arm, not here.

THE a4=r10 5-ARG FORM. agnos's 4-arg syscalls pass the 4th arg in r10 (§1a). In Cyrius source that's a 5-arg `syscall(N, a1, a2, a3, a4)` call — e.g. `sys_snd_write_nb(slot,buf,frames)` is `syscall(SYS_SND_WRITE, slot, buf, frames, 1)` where the trailing `1` = O_NONBLOCK bit0. The compiler passes that 4th syscall arg through **push → pop %r10**, so the non-blocking wrapper shares the exact instruction shape of the blocking form (`sys_symlink#63` uses the same 5-arg form) — no ABI drift between them.

The band: `SND_OPEN=64, SND_CONFIG=65, SND_WRITE=66, SND_CLOSE=67, SND_DRAIN=68, SND_AVAIL=69`; seven wrappers (write + write_nb share #66). Frozen ABI is a single 48000 Hz / 16-bit / stereo S16-interleaved output stream, slot 0..3, and #65–#69 reject a slot not owned by the caller (-1).

A PORTABLE WRAPPER THE PEER LACKS IS A LINK FAILURE — ANSWER IT WITH A DECLINE STUB, NEVER A LINUX NUMBER (v6.6.16). Because the peer is standalone, every portable `sys_*` wrapper must be re-declared there or it does not exist on agnos. `sys_setsockopt` did not: an agnos build reaching it failed the reachable-undefined check ("refusing to emit binary with N reachable undefined function(s)"), and yantra / abaco each wrapped their `TCP_NODELAY` call in an agnos `#ifdef` to compile. THE TRAP is the obvious fix: minting `SYS_SETSOCKOPT = 54` on the peer would compile and then dispatch to agnos **#54 = udp_unbind** — silently unbinding a UDP listener (yantra's pre-1.0.7 bug, when it used a raw `syscall(54, …)`). The Linux numbers land on live, unrelated agnos calls throughout the low band (#44 sched_yield, #47 sock_connect, #76/#77 blk_open/blk_read). THE RULE: a call agnos cannot honour gets an explicit `return 0 - 38;` stub in the peer's decline block, with a comment naming why and the portable alternative (here `sock_set_recv_timeout` / `sock_set_send_timeout`, which set the peer's per-socket deadlines). A crossos `.tcyr` that calls the wrapper UNGUARDED makes `tcyr_corpus_cross_compiles.sh`'s agnos leg catch the next missing one. Still absent from the peer (backlog): `sys_socket`, `sys_bind`, `sys_listen`, `sys_connect`, `sys_accept4`. See also field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml `agnos_is_an_x86_host_that_is_not_linux_and_the_predefines_do_not_say_so`.

6.7.11 — THE agnos 1.57.13 PEERS: #109 `sock_send_dl` (`SYS_SOCK_SEND_DL`, `sys_sock_send_dl` — ⚠ `timeout_us = 0` means
NO ceiling), #110 `console_mode` (`SYS_CONSOLE_MODE`, `CM_NOECHO` / `CM_FLUSH`, `sys_console_mode`; `SIGWINCH = 28` in
`AgnosSignal`), #111 `fd_kind` (`SYS_FD_KIND`, `FdKind` / `FDK_*`, `sys_fd_kind` — it answers a tagged socket itself).
The next free number is #112. A new number, not a new argument on an old one: an unused argument register carries a
STALE value on this ABI, so widening a live call's arity is not backward compatible (gotchas agnos entry, 4b).

6.7.15 — A DECLINE STUB MUST DECLINE, ON EVERY PEER (LF-5). The decline-stub rule above is not agnos-only. Through
6.7.14 Windows' `sys_dup2` was `return newfd;` under a comment claiming a reroute that did not exist: `sys_dup2(1, 50)`
"succeeded" with nothing behind fd 50, and a portable `if (sys_dup2(a, b) < 0)` never saw the failure. Since 6.7.15 it
answers -38 for every call, the self-dup `sys_dup2(1, 1)` included (the user's answer at the wave-2 integration). The PE
family fails closed: `sys_dup2` and `sys_fcntl` -38, `sys_pipe` / `sys_fork` / `sys_kill` -1. Branch on `< 0`, never on
the requested fd coming back; on Windows a child's std handles are set at spawn. CHANGELOG [6.7.15] LF-5.


[[entries]]
name = "sys_exit_is_per_thread_use_sys_exit_group"
description = "`sys_exit` is exit(2) and ends only the CALLING THREAD — a threaded program's epilogue must call `sys_exit_group` (v6.5.6, all 3 peer families). SYS_EXIT_GROUP is absent on agnos, unrouted on PE, and was an untranslated SIGSYS on macOS-x86."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.6** — filed by agnosai after a ported server would not terminate. `sys_exit` wraps **exit(2)**, which ends only the calling thread. In a single-threaded program that ends the process, so the idiomatic epilogue looked correct for years:

    var r = main();
    syscall(SYS_EXIT, r);       # ends ONE thread

The moment the program spawns its first worker — `thread_create`, a pooled server, any `chan_*` fan-out — the same line stops terminating the process: the main thread dies, the workers keep running, and it **hangs with no diagnostic**. Measured, one token apart: `syscall(SYS_EXIT, r)` → timed out (124); `sys_exit_group(r)` → 42.

WHY IT SURVIVED SO LONG. `cyrius init` shipped that epilogue in **seven** templates, so it was the default a consumer copied and then stopped looking at — and the failure only appears long after the epilogue was written, when the program grows a thread. The bug is in the *interaction*, not the line, which is why nothing flagged it.

WHY THE WRAPPER NEEDED THREE DEFINITIONS, NOT ONE. `lib/syscalls_linux_common.cyr` is included by only three peers — Linux-x86, Linux-aarch64, and **macOS-x86** (`syscalls_macos.cyr:306`). Windows and agnos are STANDALONE. A single definition there would have left `sys_exit_group` undefined on two targets, so a portable epilogue could not name it:

    syscalls_linux_common  -> SYS_EXIT_GROUP   231 x86 / 94 aarch64
    syscalls_windows       -> SYS_EXIT         ExitProcess ALREADY ends every thread;
                                               231 has no PE reroute and would emit a
                                               raw 0F 05 (illegal instruction)
    syscalls_x86_64_agnos  -> SYS_EXIT         agnos defines no exit_group; #0 tears
                                               down the whole proc

macOS-arm64 needs nothing: `lib/syscalls.cyr` routes it to the **aarch64** peer, so it resolves 94, and `src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr` already maps `94 -> BSD 1`.

THE HOLE THIS EXPOSED. macOS-**x86** resolves `SYS_EXIT_GROUP` to 231 and `EMACHO_SYSXLAT` had **no 231 entry**, so the raw number reached Darwin *unclassed* (class 0 = `SYSCALL_CLASS_NONE`) and the kernel killed the process with SIGSYS. It was silent at compile time as well: the "syscall not routed" warning is gated on `_TARGET_MACHO == 2`, i.e. **arm64 only**, so an x86-Mach-O build compiled clean and died at runtime. Fixed with one table entry (`231 -> 0x2000001`) beside the existing `60 -> 1`. The general lesson: a constant being *defined* on a target does not mean it is *routed* there — for Mach-O and PE, grep the translation table, not the enum.

Gate: `tests/exit_group_wrapper.sh`. Its axis 2 asserts the OLD epilogue **still hangs** — without that, axis 1 would silently stop proving anything the day the premise changed.

[[entries]]
name = "raw_syscall_numbers_on_aarch64_and_the_darwin_at_flag_divergence"
description = "On aarch64, a raw `syscall(N, …)` is NOT the number the kernel sees — ESYSXLAT rewrites a band of x86-compat numbers, so the native `chdir` (49) ran `bind` and the native `fchownat` (54) ran `setsockopt`. v6.5.7 opened a cyrius-private alias band at **≥1000** (`1000 + native number`) for the cases where BOTH the native and the x86 number are already owned. Separately, the `AT_*` FLAGS diverge on Darwin — only `AT_FDCWD` had ever been corrected — so a Linux-valued flag reaching Darwin is simply invalid: EINVAL, forever, silently."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.7.** Two distinct traps, found the same way: by adding ONE
`syscall_wrappers.tcyr` to the release gate's cross-OS leg so the
wrappers were finally RUN on ecb / ach instead of only compiled.
⚠ It lives at `tests/tcyr/crossos/syscall_wrappers.tcyr` — the `vr01_` prefix
this entry originally named was retired at v6.5.11 and the DIRECTORY is now the
selector; a `vr01_`-named file is no longer picked up at all.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
1. A raw `syscall(N, …)` on aarch64 is not the number the kernel sees
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

`ESYSXLAT` (`src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr`) rewrites a chain of
x86-compat source numbers so that 51 ecosystem repos emitting raw
`syscall(54, …)` and friends keep working on ARM. The consequence is
that some **aarch64-NATIVE** numbers are already spoken for as x86
source numbers, and a native-numbered call is silently re-aimed:

    source 49  → 200   # x86-compat bind      — so native chdir(49)     ran bind
    source 54  → 208   # x86-compat setsockopt— so native fchownat(54)  ran setsockopt

The documented escape has always been "use the OTHER architecture's
number plus a pure-renumber ESYSXLAT row". For these two there was no
number left to borrow: x86's 80 is this peer's own `SYS_FSTAT`, and
x86's 260 is its own `SYS_WAIT4` (native, must pass through
untranslated).

THE ANSWER — **the private alias band.** Source numbers **≥1000** are
cyrius-private aliases no OS will ever mint (Linux is in the 400s and
grows ~5/yr; Darwin is routed separately), renumbered to their true
target by ESYSXLAT. Convention is `1000 + the native aarch64 number`,
so the alias documents itself:

    SYS_FCHOWNAT = 1054     # → 54 on aarch64-Linux, → 468 on Darwin
    SYS_CHDIR    = 1049     # → 49 on aarch64-Linux, → 12  on Darwin

⛔ **Adding an alias needs THREE edits, not one**: an ESYSXLAT arm on
the aarch64-**Linux** branch, one on the **macho** branch, AND a
`_macho_arm_routes` row — because macOS-arm64 resolves this same peer.
Two out of three is a stale-`x16` garbage call.

⚠ **The aarch64-Linux arm MUST sit LAST in its chain.** It produces
`x8 = 54`, and the `setsockopt` entry earlier in that chain compares
against 54 and would re-catch it — every `fchownat` silently reissued
as `setsockopt`. Mutation-verified: the misordered build returns
`ENOPROTOOPT`. Same ordering trap already documented on flock / poll /
epoll_wait.

PRACTICAL RULE FOR CONSUMERS: **do not emit raw `syscall(N, …)` with a
number you read off a Linux man page.** Use the named `SYS_*` constant
from the peer, or a `sys_*` wrapper. The constant is the only thing
that knows about the translation chain — and note the failure mode is
never a compile error and never a signal, just a different syscall
returning a plausible errno.

**6.7.12 — IT WARNS NOW, IN YOUR OWN FILE ONLY (DW-1).** In a native-aarch64
region a raw number that is also an x86-compat row source is STILL
renumbered: `syscall(9, …)` runs mmap, and `syscall(39, …)` returns a
PID. Since 6.7.12 the compiler WARNS when that literal sits in the call's
own file, and names the `1000+N` spelling (`raw syscall 9 inside a
native-aarch64 region is renumbered → 222 (mmap); write 1009 for native
lgetxattr`). Write that alias, or the `SYS_*` name. In 6.7.12 / 6.7.13 it
did NOT warn for a per-arch file you `include` under the guard, or for a
constant you declare there.

**6.7.14 — THE REGION CARRIES THROUGH `include`, AND YOUR CONSTANT IS JUDGED
WHERE IT IS DECLARED (PL-6, 6.7.14's D61).** A per-arch file you include from inside
`#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` is under the guard, so its raw literals are
judged and the warning names the included file (`warning:src/arch_aarch64.cyr:
2:45: raw syscall 9 inside a native-aarch64 region is renumbered → 222 (mmap);
write 1009 for native lgetxattr`). An `enum` constant or a top-level `const`
(bare or `Enum.NAME`) passed to `syscall()` is judged by its DECLARATION —
declared inside a region opened in its own file, it warns `syscall constant
NR_UMOUNT2 = 39, declared inside a native-aarch64 region, is renumbered → 172
(getpid); write 1039 for native umount2` (it ran getpid silently); declared
outside one, it is silent wherever it is used. Spell x86 numbers by `SYS_*`
name and native ones as `1000 + N`. Not judged: `(9)` / `9 + 0`. Two shapes
were still silent in 6.7.14 — a fn-local `const` declared in a region, and a
constant declared at the top level of a per-arch file included under the
guard (the stdlib peers' own shape); 6.7.15 judges both (below). Still
warn-only (6.7.12's D1): no binary changes. The stdlib now names its deliberate x86
numbers (`SYS_POLL` 7, aarch64's `SYS_EPOLL_WAIT` 232), so every `lib/*.cyr`
builds for aarch64 with 0 warnings — and do not declare those names yourself
(semantics_runtime.cyml last_definition_wins_reaches_the_stdlibs_own_callers_v6714).

**6.7.15 — YOUR PER-ARCH FILE'S CONSTANTS, AND A FN-LOCAL `const`, ARE JUDGED
(6.7.15's D39; PL-9, PL-10).** A constant declared at the top level of a
per-arch file you `include` from inside `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64`
(`enum ArchNr { NR_UMOUNT2 = 39; }`) ran getpid with no warning while the
literal 39 beside it warned; since 6.7.15 it is judged by the region it is
included under, for a file OUTSIDE `lib/`. A file under `lib/` — the stdlib,
a project's vendored deps — keeps the file-local rule (it still warns for a
region of its own), so a program that includes the stdlib stays at 0
warnings. A fn-local `const NR_UMOUNT2 = 39;` declared inside a region warns
too (it was never recorded). Both warn only. CHANGELOG [6.7.15] PL-9, PL-10.

**6.7.15 — x86 33 (dup2) IS dup3 ON aarch64 LINUX, AND dup3 IS NOT dup2 FOR
THE SELF-DUP (LF-1).** aarch64 Linux has no dup2: x86 33 is routed to
`dup3(old, new, 0)`, and `dup3(fd, fd, 0)` is -EINVAL where `dup2(fd, fd)`
answers `fd` (open) or -EBADF (closed). Through 6.7.14 `sys_dup2(1, 1)`
answered -22 on aarch64 Linux, and so did a cx guest's dup2 on an aarch64
cxvm. Since 6.7.15 the WRAPPER asks `fcntl(F_GETFD)` for the self-dup (musl's
shape; glibc's generic dup2 asks `F_GETFL`, with the same answer), so
`sys_dup2(fd, fd)` is `fd` or -9 on every POSIX target. A raw
`syscall(33, fd, fd)` still reaches dup3 and answers -22: call `sys_dup2`, or
branch on `old == new` yourself. CHANGELOG [6.7.15] LF-1.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
2. The `AT_*` flags diverge on Darwin — and only the dirfd was fixed
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

`AT_FDCWD` was corrected to **-2** on macOS when ports found it. The
FLAGS were left Linux-valued for another two-and-a-half minors. Live
values (`lib/syscalls_linux_common.cyr`, read off Darwin's
`sys/fcntl.h` on ecb):

    constant              Darwin   Linux
    AT_FDCWD                  -2    -100
    AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW     0x20   0x100
    AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW       0x40   0x400
    AT_REMOVEDIR            0x80   0x200
    AT_EMPTY_PATH          (none)  0x1000   ← Darwin has NO equivalent

The at-family entries are **pure renumbers** — they pass their flags
straight through — so a Linux value on Darwin is not "wrong-ish", it is
an invalid flag: EINVAL, no warning, no fault, forever. Measured on
real ecb: `xrmdir` had **never** worked on macOS-arm64 since it shipped
at v6.5.2, and `sys_fchownat` returned -22 the first time it was ever
run there.

⭐ **THE THROUGH-LINE, worth more than either fact.** Five of the seven
defects that release were **half-fixes that stopped at the first
symptom**: `AT_FDCWD` without its flags; `STAT_SIZE` corrected without
its sibling offsets (so every `STAT_MODE` test on Intel-Mac was
silently false, reading the low half of `st_ino`); `unlink` mapped
without `rmdir`, which is the SAME syscall distinguished only by
`AT_REMOVEDIR`; three of four link syscalls mapped. Each looked
finished because the thing that prompted it started working. When you
fix one constant or one route in a family, fix the family.

AND THE COMPANION RULE, from the entry above: **a constant being
DEFINED on a target does not mean it is ROUTED there.** For Mach-O and
PE, grep the translation table, not the enum. The "syscall not routed"
warning is gated on `_TARGET_MACHO == 2` — arm64 only — so an
x86-Mach-O build compiles clean and dies at runtime.

**6.7.10 — A RAW-`asm` EXIT IS PER OS, not per architecture.** ESYSXLAT
and the Mach-O / PE routes rewrite the `syscall(N, …)` BUILTIN; a raw
`asm` block is emitted as written. An x86 `syscall` / aarch64 `svc #0`
exit_group is an exit on Linux only: arm64 macOS takes the call number
from x16 (whatever it holds), and on Windows `syscall` with eax 231 is
NT system call 0xE7 with whatever r10 / rdx / r8 / r9 hold. The
execution-coverage fixture's `asm_test.cyr` therefore runs on pi only.
The same split shows in the numbers the builtin itself treats as an
exit: raw 231 is exit_group on x86 / cx, `munlockall` on aarch64 Linux,
and unrouted (-38, the program goes on) on PE.

**6.7.12 — THE DIRFD ITSELF WAS DROPPED ON arm64 macOS (WN-3).** The
at-call route there shifted the arguments onto the BSD path call, so
`sys_openat(dirfd, rel, …)` opened `rel` against the CWD. Since 6.7.12
`sys_openat` keeps its dirfd (Darwin `openat` 463), and a raw
`syscall(56, -100, …)` still means the cwd (translated to Darwin's -2).
`sys_mkdirat` / `sys_fchmodat` there still dropped the dirfd through 6.7.13
(`mkdirat(dirfd, "x")` made `./x` in the cwd and returned 0 on ecb).

**6.7.14 — DARWIN HAS THE AT-FAMILY, WITH LINUX'S ARGUMENT ORDER (PL-5).**
openat 463, mkdirat 475, fchmodat 467, unlinkat 472: since 6.7.14
`sys_mkdirat` / `sys_fchmodat` keep their dirfd on arm64 macOS too (a
relative name used to land in the cwd, silently, rc 0) — no absolute-path
workaround needed. ⚠ fchmodat's FLAGS now reach Darwin: `AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW`
(0x20 there) chmods the link itself, while Linux's fchmodat syscall ignores
the flags word and follows the link; a raw Linux 0x100 is -22 on macOS (the
table above). CHANGELOG [6.7.12] WN-3, [6.7.14] PL-5, PL-6.

**6.7.15 — THE `O_*` FLAGS DIVERGE TOO (LF-8).** The open flags are per
target, like the `AT_*` ones:

    flag          x86_64 Linux   aarch64 Linux   Darwin
    O_CREAT           0x40           0x40         0x200
    O_TRUNC          0x200          0x200         0x400
    O_EXCL            0x80           0x80         0x800
    O_NOFOLLOW     0x20000         0x8000         0x100

Linux's `577` (O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC) is O_WRONLY | O_ASYNC | O_CREAT
on Darwin: it does not truncate, so a reused file keeps its old tail after a
shorter write. lib/regression.cyr's capture file overwrote the head of a
planted link's target that way on macOS (CYRIUS-2026-0036: a_file_at_a_
predictable_name_in_a_shared_directory_is_safe_only_when_created_exclusively),
and its caller-path verbs read "hi\ncious\n" on ecb / ach where Linux reads
"hi\n" (fixed in 6.7.16, LF-1: `regression_exec_in_dir3[_env]`,
`regression_pipe_to_bin_capture` and `regression_ssh_remote_exec_capture`
open `out_path` with each target's `O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC`). Spell open
flags with the `O_*` constants — lib/syscalls_*.cyr define each target's
bits. THE RULE, portable and not:
  - a numeric open-flag literal is a LINUX-bits literal: 577, 64, 512 and
    1024 mean other flags on Darwin, where O_CREAT is 0x200, O_TRUNC 0x400
    and O_APPEND 0x8, and 0x40 is O_ASYNC;
  - only 0, 1 and 2 — the access mode — are portable; spell the rest by name;
  - `regression_child_fd_shapes.sh` rule `flags` holds lib/regression.cyr to
    it; lib/regression_agnos.cyr's `file_open(…, 577, …)` is right only because
    agnos's `file_open` maps the neutral Linux-shaped bits.
CHANGELOG [6.7.15] LF-8; [6.7.16] LF-1.

[[entries]]
name = "the_stdlib_now_names_the_send_side_truncate_memfd_and_the_scheduler_pair"
description = "v6.6.5. sys_sendmsg / sys_sendto / sys_ftruncate / sys_truncate / sys_memfd_create / sys_nanosleep / sys_sched_yield exist in lib/syscalls.cyr on every target. Before them a consumer had to hardcode the x86_64 number, and on ELF-aarch64 that runs a DIFFERENT, VALID syscall with a clean, silent build — raw 77 ran tee, raw 46 ran ftruncate, raw 44 ran fstatfs."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["syscalls", "aarch64", "esysxlat", "portability", "wayland", "shm", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**WHAT TO WRITE.** The names, always:

    var fd = sys_memfd_create("buf", 1);        # MFD_CLOEXEC; -78 on macOS, < 0 on Windows
    sys_ftruncate(fd, w * h * 4);
    sys_sendmsg(sock, &msghdr, 0);              # SCM_RIGHTS fd-passing
    sys_sendto(sock, buf, n, 0, &addr, alen);
    sys_truncate(path, len);
    sys_nanosleep(&timespec, 0);                # PE -> Sleep, macOS -> poll, agnos -> sleep_ms
    sys_sched_yield();

⛔ **WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN AN ERGONOMIC WIN.** ELF-aarch64 passes an unrecognised syscall
number straight to the `svc` — it has to, so native numbers survive. So the x86 number is not
an error there, it is a different call. Measured under `qemu-aarch64 -strace` before v6.6.5:

| you wrote | you meant | aarch64 ran |
|---|---|---|
| 319 | memfd_create | ENOSYS |
| 77 | ftruncate | **tee** |
| 46 | sendmsg | **ftruncate** |
| 44 | sendto | fstatfs |
| 35 | nanosleep | unlinkat(0, NULL, 0) |
| 87 / 84 / 83 / 89 / 33 | unlink / rmdir / mkdir / readlink / dup2 | timerfd_gettime / sync_file_range / fdatasync / acct / mknodat |

v6.6.5 routes all of those, so the x86 spelling now WORKS as well — but the name is still the
answer, because six numbers stay unrouted on purpose: **24** (`sched_yield` on x86 is **dup3**
on aarch64), 53 (socketpair / fchmodat), 52 (getpeername / fchmod), 21 (access / epoll_ctl),
90 (chmod / capget) and 17 (pread64 / getcwd). A compat row for any of those would break the
native call somebody already uses.

⚠ **DO NOT WRITE THE AARCH64-NATIVE NUMBER UNDER AN `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64`.** It looks
like the careful thing and it is the fragile one: a routing row matches a NUMBER and cannot
tell your native number from the x86 number it is chasing. Three ecosystem repos had exactly
that shape when v6.6.5 landed and all three break at their pin bump — thoth's `= 46` (meant as
native ftruncate) becomes sendmsg, kybernet's `= 45` (truncate) becomes recvfrom, and attn11's
`n = 83` (fdatasync) becomes mkdirat. ⚠ A **variable** number is rewritten too: the chain runs
on whatever is in `x8` at run time, so `var n = 83; syscall(n, fd);` is not an escape.

**OFF-LINUX, the wrappers do the right thing rather than the same thing.** memfd declines with
-78 on macOS (Darwin has no memfd_create; shm_open is a different object model) and < 0 on
Windows. nanosleep and sched_yield compose from `poll` on macOS, route to kernel32 `Sleep` on
PE, and use `sleep_ms` on agnos. The agnos peer declares **no Linux constants at all** for
these: #44 is its sched_yield, #46 time_unix, #47 sock_connect, #76 blk_open and #77 blk_read,
so a minted `SYS_FTRUNCATE = 77` there would issue a raw block-device read.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "the_clock_you_get_per_target_is_not_the_clock_you_asked_for"
description = "6.6.5. Darwin CLOCK_MONOTONIC (id 6) is gettimeofday minus boottime with MICROSECOND resolution; Windows had only GetTickCount64's 15 ms tick until QPC; AGNOS has no syscall 228 at all. Three targets where a 'monotonic nanosecond clock' was not one."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["platform", "clock", "macos", "windows", "agnos", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`now_ns()` and `clock_now_ns()` look portable and were not. Measured per target:

  **macOS.** Passing Darwin clock id **6** (`CLOCK_MONOTONIC`) gets you
  `gettimeofday - boottime` in Apple's own Libc (`gen/clock_gettime.c`), whose `clock_getres` is
  `NSEC_PER_USEC` — **microsecond** resolution. That is exactly the 1,000 ns tick recorded on
  both ecb and ach for a year. Use **4** (`CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW` → `mach_continuous_time`), which
  `lib/sakshi.cyr` and `src/backend/common/runtime.cyr`'s `_prof_clock_ns` already did. The
  arm64 route RETURNS ns in the return register and does NOT fill the timespec; x86-macOS drops
  the id entirely and composes µs from `gettimeofday`, a documented platform ceiling.

  **Windows.** `syscall(228)` routed to kernel32!**GetTickCount64**, whose counter advances in
  **15 ms** steps (measured on real cass, not quoted). Every duration under 15 ms read as 0 or
  as one whole tick — so no cyrius program could time anything on Windows, and the bench
  framework's floor calibrated to 0 because 1,024 calls do not move the counter. 6.6.5 adds the
  PE reroutes **0xF038 / 0xF039** (QueryPerformanceCounter / Frequency) and
  `sys_qpc_ns()`; GetTickCount64 remains as the fallback and as an INDEPENDENT clock for tests.

  **AGNOS.** There is no syscall 228. A build emitted a raw `movl $0xe4,%eax; syscall` into a
  kernel that does not define it, so the timespec read back was undefined. Use `#95`
  `sys_uptime_us` (rdtsc-derived, needs no interrupts — `#40 uptime_ms` is FROZEN in a
  foreground program because IF is cleared).

⚠ **A COST and a TICK are different numbers, and on three of five targets the tick is larger.**
Measured: linux 1,332 ns cost / 1,397 ns tick; ecb 11-39 / 1,000 (pre-fix); ach 540-571 / 1,000;
pi 3,691 / 3,723; cass 0 / 15,000,000. Anything sizing a measurement window from the cost alone
sizes for nothing on a coarse counter.

⭐ **And the general lesson is about the SECOND caller.** `lib/chrono.cyr` was moved to `#95` at
6.6.1; `lib/bench.cyr` has the same `now_ns` and was not, for four releases. A correction applied
to one clock caller is not a correction. Both are now pinned on one gate axis
(`tests/gates/platform/agnos_monotonic_clock_rdtsc.sh`), with an objdump emission check and the
Linux build as the anti-vacuous control.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "routing_a_syscall_number_is_only_half_of_portability_the_struct_width_is_the_other_half"
description = "6.6.6. Every syscall-portability note in this repo is about the NUMBER. statfs showed the second half: Darwin's f_bsize is uint32 with f_iosize packed above it while Linux's is a full 8 bytes, so a shared offset table is still wrong — load64 returns f_bsize | (f_iosize << 32)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["platform", "syscall", "esysxlat", "macos", "aarch64", "statfs", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
The `statfs` repair at 6.6.6 had the familiar shape on the way in — aarch64-Linux's native
`statfs` is **43**, ESYSXLAT's x86-compat row `accept 43→202` was reissuing it, and under
`qemu-aarch64 -strace` a `syscall(43, "/", &buf)` traced as
`accept(6293449, 0x7f…, [0]) = -1 errno=14`. That part is the documented shadow class: declare
the OTHER arch's number and add a row (137→43), below the row that claims your product.

⭐ **The part that was NOT documented anywhere is the struct.** Getting the number right lands
you in a buffer whose layout differs per OS — and this repo already knew about differing
OFFSETS (`STAT_MODE` is +24 on x86_64-Linux, +16 on aarch64-Linux, +4 on Darwin arm64). What
`statfs` adds is a differing **WIDTH**:

  Linux `struct statfs` (both arches, 120 B):  f_type@0(8)  f_bsize@8(8)   f_blocks@16 …
  Darwin `struct statfs64`      (2168 B):      f_bsize@0(**4**)  f_iosize@4(4)  f_blocks@8 …

So `load64(buf + STATFS_BSIZE)` on macOS returns `f_bsize | (f_iosize << 32)` —
**4503599627374592** for a 4096-byte block on a 1 MiB-iosize volume, read off ecb. A caller
multiplies that by a block count and reports a capacity. It never faults, never errors, and
every offset in the table was *correct*.

The answer is an **accessor, not a constant**: peers publish `statfs_bsize(buf)`, which is
`load32` on Darwin and `load64` elsewhere. When a field's width differs across peers, an offset
enum cannot express it and a comment will not stop anyone.

⚠ Two smaller lessons from the same bite, both about notes going stale rather than being wrong:

  * The agnos peer had written an **honest gap** note at v6.5.37 — "`sys_statfs` exists NOWHERE
    else in this repo … the alternative was to invent statfs numbers for four more targets from
    memory … an honest gap beats a plausible wrong number" — and recorded the upgrade path to
    take when a cross-platform consumer appeared. The reasoning was right and the note became
    FALSE the moment the consumer landed. An honest-gap note needs its trigger written into it,
    and someone has to notice the trigger fired: yukti had been the consumer all along, silently
    broken on ARM for four minors.
  * A syscall with **no NAME in the peers** is invisible to the raw-literal diagnostic, because
    that table is derived from names declared in BOTH peers. So "nobody has named it yet" is not
    a neutral state — it is the state in which consumers hardcode numbers and nothing warns.
    Naming the call is the fix; the ESYSXLAT row is only what makes the name correct.

Numbers, all MEASURED rather than recalled (the rule that note demanded): Linux 137/138 x86_64,
43/44 aarch64; Darwin **statfs64 345 / fstatfs64 346**, live-probed on ecb and ach against libc
(the legacy `statfs(157)` fills a different, shifted struct); Windows has none, so the wrapper
declines with -38. Pinned by `tests/gates/platform/statfs_family_routed.sh` and
`tests/tcyr/crossos/statfs_family.tcyr`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "pollout_promises_pipe_buf_not_the_whole_write"
description = "6.6.6. A blocking write larger than the pipe does not come back short — it waits until every byte is delivered — so poll(POLLOUT) then write(n) blocks on byte 65537 exactly as an unpolled write does. POLLOUT on a pipe promises PIPE_BUF (4096) bytes of room and nothing more."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["platform", "pipe", "poll", "syscall", "deadline", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
Bounding a pump that feeds a child's stdin looks like this, and it is wrong:

    if (_poll_fd(fd, 4, timeout_ms) <= 0) { return written; }   # POLLOUT
    var w = sys_write(fd, buf + written, n - written);          # ... the whole remainder

It was measured RED in 6.6.6 with a 1 MB source piped into a child that never reads: the
poll returns ready (the pipe is empty), the write fills the 64 KB buffer and then **blocks
for ever**, because a blocking `write(2)` of more than the pipe can hold does not return a
short count — it waits until the whole request is delivered. A short count comes back only
when a signal handler interrupts a write that has already transferred something.

What POLLOUT actually promises on a pipe is that a write of up to **PIPE_BUF (4096)** bytes
will not block. So the chunk is the bound:

    var chunk_cap = n;
    if (timeout_ms > 0) { chunk_cap = 4096; }   # PIPE_BUF — what POLLOUT promises
    ... var chunk = n - written; if (chunk > chunk_cap) { chunk = chunk_cap; }

The READ side does not need this: `read(2)` returns as soon as one byte is available, so a
poll-then-read of any size cannot block.

Two more things the same bite measured, worth having before you write one of these:

  * **The deadline must be on the PUMP as well as the WAIT.** Every fork+exec verb in
    `lib/regression.cyr`, `lib/process.cyr` and `programs/checks/` pumps the child's pipe
    BEFORE it waits, so a wait deadline alone never runs — the runner is already blocked in
    `sys_read`/`sys_write`. `tests/gates/toolchain/check_driver_bounded.sh` axis 2d pins it.
  * **Writing into a child that already exited kills the WRITER with SIGPIPE**, default
    disposition, no diagnostic — exit 141 from a runner that looks like it crashed. That is
    unrelated to the deadline and is still open in this tree (pre-6.6.6 behaviour); a pump
    with no SIGPIPE handling reports a dead child as its own death. One instance of the class
    in `a_write_to_a_reset_peer_raises_sigpipe_and_a_library_may_neither_rely_on_nor_change_the_disposition`.

`syscall(7, &pfd, 1, ms)` is the portable spelling: the backend translates 7 to Darwin poll
(230) and to aarch64 `ppoll` (73) with the ms->timespec shift, which is why `lib/net.cyr`
has used that literal since v6.2.10.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_rooted_posix_path_is_drive_relative_on_windows_and_any_local_user_can_plant_it"
description = "CLASS — FOUR CVEs in five releases (6.6.11 to 6.6.15), each found one module at a time. On Windows a rooted path such as /etc/hosts resolves against the CURRENT drive (C:\\etc\\hosts), and any authenticated user may create a folder at the root of the system drive, so a stdlib that opens a POSIX path on every target lets one local user answer every other user's program. CYRIUS-2026-0012 (6.6.11, hosts + resolv.conf), CYRIUS-2026-0014 (6.6.12, sandhi's resolver), CYRIUS-2026-0020 (6.6.14, the TLS trust store), CYRIUS-2026-0024 (6.6.15, sigil's TPM / Secure Boot / IMA / dm-verity / LUKS helpers — a planted file answered trust questions and received a LUKS key). Two of the four were in FOLDED stdlib. The loader's DLL search order is the same planting, one level down"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["platform", "windows", "pe", "security", "path-planting", "dll-search-order", "crypt32", "callptr", "sigil", "tpm", "fail-closed", "CYRIUS-2026-0012", "CYRIUS-2026-0014", "CYRIUS-2026-0020", "CYRIUS-2026-0024", "v6.6.11", "v6.6.12", "v6.6.14", "v6.6.15"]
---
**THE MECHANISM.** `file_open` hands a rooted path to `CreateFileW`, and Windows resolves
`\etc\...` against the current drive — `C:\etc\ssl\cert.pem` for a program started on C:.
Creating `C:\etc` needs no privilege. Nothing fails and nothing warns: the file is absent until
someone plants it, and from then on it is trusted.

| CVE | release | opened on Windows | what the planter controlled |
|---|---|---|---|
| 54 | 6.6.11 | `/etc/hosts`, `/etc/resolv.conf` (`lib/net.cyr`), plus a fallback nameserver 127.0.0.1:53 — port 53 is not privileged on Windows | every other user's name resolution |
| 57 | 6.6.12 | `/etc/resolv.conf` (the folded sandhi resolver) | every other user's DNS server |
| 65 | 6.6.14 | `/etc/ssl/cert.pem` + three sibling bundles (`_tn_ca_read`) | every other user's TLS trust anchors — measured on cass, a planted `cert.pem` became the ONLY root |
| 73 | 6.6.15 (sigil 3.13.9) | `/dev/tpmrm0`, the IMA securityfs files, `/sys/module/dm_verity`, `/usr/bin/mokutil`, a `/tmp` staging dir | "is there a TPM", "what did IMA measure" (and `ima_write_policy` WROTE into the plant), "is dm-verity available" — and `luks_write_keyfile` staged a LUKS key in a directory the planter owns |

**CYRIUS-2026-0024 was listed here as "still open" at 6.6.14 — for the TPM helpers only.** The fix found
the class in EVERY sigil trust core. Its shape is the reusable one when a POSIX mechanism has no
Windows equivalent at all: one predicate (`agnosys_rooted_paths_untrusted()`, 1 on Windows) asked
before every probe of a fixed rooted path, and each probe FAILS CLOSED — `tpm_detect` 0,
`secureboot_detect_state` not-supported, `ima_get_status` inactive, the operations Err — rather
than answering from whatever is there. Not covered by any of the four fixes: a rooted path a
CALLER passes in on Windows (`tpm_seal`'s output dir, an EFI variable path) — the caller's to
resolve.

**THE RULE.** A rooted path literal in `lib/` is a POSIX-only path: it sits under
`#ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN`, and the Windows arm asks the OS (getaddrinfo, the adapters' DNS
servers, the certificate store) or fails closed. Audit by grepping `lib/` AND every folded dep's
SOURCE for `"/` literals — two of the four (sandhi, sigil) were in folds, and each of the four was
found one module at a time, by whoever happened to be in it; a fifth module is likelier than not.
⚠ Wine does NOT show the class (it maps a rooted path to the unix root); only real Windows (cass)
proves it. A test plants the file itself at the exact path the old code opened, asserts the plant
exists, and removes only what it created — or, to plant every path without touching C:, maps a
scratch directory to a drive letter with `subst` and runs from that drive (sigil
`scripts/cass-rooted-paths.sh`).

**THE SAME PLANTING THROUGH THE DLL SEARCH ORDER.** An IAT import of a DLL that is not a KnownDLL
(`HKLM\...\Session Manager\KnownDLLs`: kernel32, ws2_32 and shell32 are; crypt32, dxgi and
bcryptprimitives are not) is looked for in the APPLICATION DIRECTORY first, so a `crypt32.dll`
dropped beside the program runs at its load. CYRIUS-2026-0020's store reader therefore does not import
crypt32: it loads it from System32 at first use, with no compiler change at all —

    var k  = syscall(0xF013, "kernel32.dll");          # GetModuleHandleA
    var ll = syscall(0xF014, k, "LoadLibraryExA");      # GetProcAddress
    var c  = callptr(ll, "crypt32.dll", 0, 0x800);      # LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_SYSTEM32
    var f  = syscall(0xF014, c, "CertOpenStore");        # then callptr(f, ...): 5+ args go on the stack

— and it keeps crypt32 out of the startup of every program that never verifies. Mask a BOOL /
DWORD result (`& 0xFFFFFFFF`: the upper half is undefined) and read `GetLastError` (0xF04B)
straight after the call it explains. CHANGELOG [6.6.11] [6.6.12] [6.6.14] [6.6.15]; sigil `docs/audit/2026-10-03-3.13.9-pin-bump-windows-rooted-paths-audit.md` §F8.

[[entries]]
name = "a_file_at_a_predictable_name_in_a_shared_directory_is_safe_only_when_created_exclusively"
description = "CLASS — the POSIX half of the planting class (its Windows half: a_rooted_posix_path_is_drive_relative_on_windows_and_any_local_user_can_plant_it). A file a program writes at a PREDICTABLE name in a shared directory (`/tmp/x_<pid>`, `<path>.tmp.<pid>.<n>`) and opens `O_CREAT | O_TRUNC` follows a symlink another local user planted there, so the victim's write lands in a file the planter chose. Found one module at a time: the 2026-09-03 audit (scripts/, cbt/), CYRIUS-2026-0034 (6.6.20, lib/io.cyr's atomic-write temp), CYRIUS-2026-0036 (6.7.15, lib/regression.cyr's capture file). Open `O_CREAT | O_EXCL | O_NOFOLLOW`, 0600, with each target's `O_*` bits; skip a taken name, bounded; use an absolute `$TMPDIR`. Linux's protected_symlinks hides the cross-user case — macOS does not. And an exclusive file unlinked only after use leaks for ever when its process is SIGKILLed: unlink it at the create and keep the fd (6.7.16)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["platform", "security", "linux", "macos", "windows", "/tmp", "TMPDIR", "O_EXCL", "O_NOFOLLOW", "O_RDWR", "unlink", "SIGKILL", "symlink", "path-planting", "temp files", "regression.cyr", "io.cyr", "check.sh", "protected_symlinks", "CYRIUS-2026-0034", "CYRIUS-2026-0036", "v6.6.20", "v6.7.15", "v6.7.16"]
---
THE MECHANISM. A name another local user can predict (a pid, a counter), in a directory every user can write (a sticky
`/tmp`, a shared build directory), opened `O_CREAT | O_TRUNC`: the planter creates a symlink at the next name first, and
the open FOLLOWS it, so the victim's write lands in whatever file the link names that the VICTIM can write — overwriting
it, or its head where the flags do not truncate (Linux's 577 on Darwin: raw_syscall_numbers_on_aarch64_and_the_darwin_
at_flag_divergence, part 2). Nothing fails and nothing warns. THE INSTANCES, each found one module at a time:
  - the 2026-09-03 audit: scripts/ and cbt/'s temp files — now `_cbt_tmpdir` / `_cbt_tmpfile`;
  - CYRIUS-2026-0034 (6.6.20): lib/io.cyr's atomic-write temp `<path>.cyrtmp.<pid>.<ctr>`;
  - CYRIUS-2026-0036 (P2, 6.7.15): lib/regression.cyr's capture file `/tmp/audit_x?_<pid>`, behind the six
    `regression_exec_*capture*` verbs (attacker: another local user; boundary: the shared `/tmp`).

THE RULE.
  - Open `O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_EXCL | O_NOFOLLOW` — `O_RDWR` when the creator reads the file back — mode 0600, spelled
    with each target's `O_*` constants — a numeric literal is right on one target only. `O_EXCL` alone already refuses a
    final symlink; `O_NOFOLLOW` says so in the source, where an audit grep sees it.
  - Skip a TAKEN name (EEXIST — lib/io.cyr's `_io_tmp_taken`), with a bound (64), and refuse by name past it. The
    capture verbs then print one `regression: cannot create the capture file …` line and answer 0 bytes with status -1,
    running nothing (6.7.15).
  - Use `$TMPDIR` when it is ABSOLUTE (drop trailing slashes: macOS sets `/var/folders/…/T/`), else `/tmp`; ignore a
    relative or empty value.
  - On Windows a rooted `/tmp` is a drive-relative path another user can create: create nothing there (the capture
    verbs answer 0 / -1 and make no file), or use the OS's temp directory.

THE TRAP IN TESTING. Linux's `fs.protected_symlinks` / `fs.protected_regular` refuse ANOTHER user's plant in a sticky
`/tmp`, so a Linux dev box hides the cross-user case; a same-user plant still shows the open following the link. macOS
(`/private/tmp`) has neither setting — CYRIUS-2026-0036 was live there — so run the planted-link row on a Mac. Plant
from ANOTHER process, as the attacker does, at the names the library's own helpers predict: a plant that misses the name
proves nothing.

THE COST, AND ITS FIX (6.7.16, LF-3 – LF-5). An O_EXCL temp file unlinked only AFTER use leaks for ever when its process
is SIGKILLed: an exclusive create never reuses a name, so O_EXCL turns a leak that a reused name used to clean (O_TRUNC)
into a permanent one — in 6.7.15 every SIGKILLed capture (the check driver at a deadline, a gate's kill axis, the user)
left its `audit_x[ca]_<pid>` behind, and enough at one pid reach the 64-name bound. So:
  - Unlink at the create and keep the fd (open `O_RDWR`): the capture verbs do since 6.7.16 — the file has no name while
    the child runs, and a killed capture leaves nothing (only the open → unlink window remains).
  - When a forked child writes through a dup of it, the offset is SHARED: the parent seeks to 0 before reading back.
  - A parent that keeps such an fd open lifts it above 2 — one started with fd 2 closed opens it AT 2, and its own
    stderr writes then land in the capture (shell_runtime.cyml
    a_parent_with_a_std_fd_closed_hands_that_number_to_its_next_fd_v6715).
  - Reap older leftovers only by shape, ownership, age and a dead pid, never through a link: cyrius's check.sh does
    since 6.7.16 (a regular file the user owns, older than `CYRIUS_CHECK_REAP_MINS`, its pid dead; shell_runtime.cyml
    posix_mode_bash_kill_0_of_a_non_number_answers_0_v6716).
CYRIUS-2026-0036's guarantees are kept; LF-5 edits its open. Not a vulnerability: the leftover was the victim's own
0600 file.

AUDIT by grepping `lib/**/*.cyr` — and every folded dep's SOURCE — for `"/tmp` literals; since 6.7.15
`regression_capture_exclusive.sh` axis 4 does it, with an allowlist of two entries (`_regression_tmp_base`, sigil's
`luks_keyfile_path`). Beside it, a bug and not a vulnerability (the path is the caller's own): three regression verbs
opened a CALLER's `out_path` with the literal 577, which does not truncate on Darwin — fixed in 6.7.16 (LF-1).
CHANGELOG [6.6.20] CYRIUS-2026-0034; [6.7.15] LF-8 (CYRIUS-2026-0036); [6.7.16] LF-1, LF-3 – LF-5;
docs/audit/2026-09-03-security-audit.md.

[[entries]]
name = "tmp_and_dev_null_are_linux_fixtures_v6714"
description = "A test that opens `/tmp/x` or `/dev/null` holds a POSIX fixture: on Windows `/tmp/x` is the drive-rooted `\\tmp\\x`, which need not exist (patra 1.16.3's suite died at its first open on cass), and `/dev/null` does not exist — `NUL` opens and takes a write and a seek, but FlushFileBuffers refuses it, as it refuses a read-only handle. And an fsync / fdatasync of `/dev/null` is EINVAL on Linux but succeeds on macOS, and a pipe is no substitute. Route scratch paths through a per-target helper, and inject a sync failure through a test seam (patra 1.16.5 does both). And a file that asserts nothing on a target fails the cross-OS leg there (6.7.16)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "v6.7.15", "v6.7.16", "windows", "macos", "tests", "/tmp", "/dev/null", "NUL", "fsync", "fdatasync", "pipe", "FlushFileBuffers", "patra", "fixtures", "test seam", "0 passed", "cross-OS"]
---
- On Windows `"/tmp/x"` is the drive-rooted `\tmp\x`, which need not exist: patra 1.16.3's own suite died at its first
  open on cass. Route test scratch paths through a per-target helper; a RELATIVE name works on Windows. (The same
  resolution is the planting class in
  a_rooted_posix_path_is_drive_relative_on_windows_and_any_local_user_can_plant_it.)
- `/dev/null` does not exist on Windows. `NUL` opens and takes a write and a seek, but `FlushFileBuffers` refuses it —
  exactly as it refuses a read-only handle — so no errno tells "nothing to sync" from a real failure there: assert the
  premise and SKIP the row by name.
- On Linux an fsync / fdatasync of `/dev/null` is EINVAL; on macOS it answers 0. A "nothing to sync" fixture is
  per-target, and a PIPE is no substitute: its sync is refused on macOS (ENOTSUP), but a write path that seeks first
  fails on the pipe's seek before it ever syncs. To test a failed sync, inject the failure through a test seam — patra
  1.16.5's macOS row does (PL-15; it was filed against patra's suite at 6.7.14).
- patra 1.16.5 takes every harness's scratch path from one helper (`tests/scratch_path.cyr`, relative on Windows),
  CI-gated against a bare `/tmp` literal (PL-16).
- ⚠ A computed write offset that is harmless on Linux fills a Windows disk:
  a_write_far_past_eof_is_a_huge_real_write_on_ntfs_v6715.
- A FILE THAT ASSERTS NOTHING ON A TARGET FAILS THERE, IT IS NOT A SKIP (6.7.16, TT-1). The cross-OS runner grades `0
  passed` red (`N passed` must be at least 1): `crossos/exec_cmd_forwards_environment.tcyr` wrapped its whole body in
  `#ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN`, its header claiming Windows "reports only its summary", and the cass leg went red. Give
  each target a file runs on at least one real assertion of the same property (there, through `cmd.exe`), or keep the
  file off that target's corpus by the runner's own selection.
- An emulator is not the platform either (wine's rooted paths and empty ROOT store, qemu-user's missing calls):
  wine_and_qemu_user_are_not_the_platforms_they_emulate_v6716.
patra 1.16.4 runs its suite on Windows this way (1,565 / 1,565 on cass). CHANGELOG [6.7.14] LIB-10; [6.7.15] PL-15,
PL-16 (patra 1.16.5); [6.7.16] TT-1.

[[entries]]
name = "a_write_far_past_eof_is_a_huge_real_write_on_ntfs_v6715"
description = "A write at a huge file offset is a SPARSE file on Linux, macOS (APFS) and wine (a 4 KB write at 575783634888364032 costs 4 KB, at once), but NTFS files are not sparse by default: Windows zero-fills the whole gap in one kernel wait that TerminateProcess cannot interrupt. On cass one such write ran ~17 minutes, survived `taskkill` / `Stop-Process -Force`, and left a 50,465,939,456-byte file. A wrong offset that 'passes' off Windows fills a Windows disk: bound a test's file size."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.15", "windows", "ntfs", "sparse files", "lseek", "write", "Valid Data Length", "TerminateProcess", "wine", "macos", "patra", "fuzz", "tests"]
---
WHAT HAPPENS. A seek far past end of file, then a write: Linux, macOS (APFS) and wine on Linux make the gap a sparse
hole and return at once — `lseek(3, 575783634888364032)` then a 4 KB write costs 4 KB. NTFS files are not sparse by
default, so Windows zero-fills everything before the write (the file's Valid Data Length) in ONE kernel wait that
TerminateProcess cannot interrupt: on cass one write sat about 17 minutes, `taskkill` and `Stop-Process -Force` could
not end it, and it left a 50,465,939,456-byte file (deleted by hand).

HOW IT WAS MET (patra 1.16.5; PL-16, LF-4). patra's `fuzz_bytes.fcyr` took the chain's head page from a query result
whose `BR_PAGE` has held the payload's HEAP POINTER since patra 1.12.8, so every mutation went to `page_offset(<an
address>)`, about 5.7e17 bytes in. On Linux and macOS that was a cheap sparse write — the harness passed everywhere,
having tested nothing; once 1.16.5's scratch paths let it open its file on Windows, it hit the zero-fill. Fixed in the
same patra release: the harness plants its mutations on the chain the row names and proves each one landed.

THE RULE. Never trust a fixture that writes at a COMPUTED offset to be harmless because it passed off Windows: a wrong
offset is a sparse no-op on Linux and macOS and a disk-filling, unkillable write on Windows hardware (wine does not
show it). Bound a test's file size — assert the offset lies inside the file you made — and prove a planted mutation
landed by reading it back. tmp_and_dev_null_are_linux_fixtures_v6714 (the other Windows fixture traps).
CHANGELOG [6.7.15] PL-16, LF-4 (patra 1.16.5).

[[entries]]
name = "a_write_to_a_reset_peer_raises_sigpipe_and_a_library_may_neither_rely_on_nor_change_the_disposition"
description = "CLASS. write(2) to a socket whose peer has reset raises SIGPIPE, whose default action kills the WHOLE process — every connection of a server, on the say-so of any client that disconnects. An application may ignore the signal; a LIBRARY may neither assume it did nor change the process-wide disposition for it. Per OS: Linux MSG_NOSIGNAL per call; macOS SO_NOSIGPIPE per socket, failing CLOSED when xnu refuses it; a library that does not own the write (libssl) holds SIGPIPE off in the calling thread around the call. CYRIUS-2026-0021 (6.6.14) was both TLS backends at once; CYRIUS-2026-0025 (6.6.16) the plain-socket writers — lib/net.cyr's sock_send* (and so http, ws, ws_server, yantra, sandhi's client paths) and the async send / relay writers — now through one private leaf, `_fd_write_nosigpipe` (lib/syscalls.cyr)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["platform", "linux", "macos", "signals", "sigpipe", "sockets", "security", "seccomp", "tls", "libssl", "async", "CYRIUS-2026-0021", "CYRIUS-2026-0025", "v6.4.51", "v6.6.14", "v6.6.16"]
---
**THE TRIGGER IS ORDINARY.** The peer closes; our next write reaches a closed socket and its
kernel answers RST; the write after that is EPIPE — with SIGPIPE, unless the write said
otherwise. Measured against the 6.6.13 lib: a native TLS writer exited 141 on x86_64, pi, ecb and
ach (one of the five rows was the ClientHello, the handshake's own first write), and a libssl
SERVER died in `tls_accept_complete` answering a client that had connected and hung up.

**INSTANCES.** v6.4.51 `signal_ignore(SIGPIPE)` — right for an APPLICATION (sandhi's server
calls it), wrong inside a library. 6.6.6: a pump writing into a child that already exited dies of
it (`pollout_promises_pipe_buf_not_the_whole_write`). 6.6.14, CYRIUS-2026-0021: every native TLS write, and
every libssl call that can write. 6.6.16, CYRIUS-2026-0025: `lib/net.cyr`'s `_net_os_send` (under
`sock_send` / `sock_send_all` / `sock_send_a`, so http, ws, ws_server, yantra and sandhi's client
paths) and the async backends' `async_send` task (a raw `SYS_WRITE` on epoll) and
`async_relay_once` — all flagless `write(2)`s, all exit 141 on x86_64, qemu-aarch64, pi, ecb and
ach. They share ONE leaf now, `_fd_write_nosigpipe(fd, buf, n)` in `lib/syscalls.cyr`, so net.cyr
and both async backends cannot drift; the TLS transport keeps its per-ctx copy (`_tn_os_write` /
`_tn_nosigpipe`). 6.7.15 (W0-1): a PIPE into a child — no per-call flag exists for a pipe — killed the CALLER when the
child exited without reading its stdin: `regression_pipe_write_all` (lib/regression.cyr) took the check driver down
with 141 mid-run, every later row left without a verdict. It now ignores SIGPIPE for the length of its own write when
the disposition is SIG_DFL, and puts it back after: a caller's own ignore or handler stands, and the child, forked
before the write, inherits the caller's own disposition.

**THE FIX, PER MECHANISM** (`_tn_os_write` / `_tn_nosigpipe` in `lib/tls_native_conn.cyr`,
`_tls_ssl_call` in `lib/tls.cyr`):

- **Linux:** `sys_sendto(fd, buf, n, 0x4000 /*MSG_NOSIGNAL*/, 0, 0)`; on ENOTSOCK (-88) fall
  back to `write(2)` — a pipe or a file keeps its SIGPIPE, as no per-call flag exists for one.
- **macOS:** `setsockopt(fd, 0xFFFF, 0x1022 /*SO_NOSIGPIPE*/)` — per connection for TLS (the
  ctx caches the fd), per CALL for the plain leaf, because a socket made by a raw `sys_socket`
  (sandhi's IPv6 paths) never passes a creation hook. Darwin DOES define `MSG_NOSIGNAL` (0x80000;
  measured working on ach's 13.7.8 and ecb's 27.0.1 — the SDK header guards it with
  `__DARWIN_C_LEVEL >= 200809L`, which is where "macOS has no MSG_NOSIGNAL" came from, and this
  entry said so until 6.6.16); it is not relied on because `SO_NOSIGPIPE` works on every Darwin and
  is what libraries there use. The option set on a LISTENER is inherited by the accepted socket,
  and a bit set before an RST keeps protecting after it. ⛔ But xnu REFUSES to set it (EINVAL) on a
  socket shut down both ways — exactly the state an RST leaves — so "set it, ignore failure,
  write" is the hole itself: any failure but ENOTSOCK (38) fails the write closed (the plain leaf
  answers -EPIPE and writes NOTHING, the errno the write would have given). A mutant ignoring the
  failure read 141 on ecb and ach.
- **A write you do not own** (OpenSSL's socket BIO calls `write(2)`; Linux has no per-socket
  flag): libpq's pattern, per call — `rt_sigprocmask(SIG_BLOCK, {SIGPIPE}, &old)`; if SIGPIPE was
  ALREADY blocked, note with `rt_sigpending` whether one was pending (that one is the caller's);
  make the call; if none was pending before and one is now, take it with a zero-timeout
  `rt_sigtimedwait`; restore `old`. Consume unconditionally — without errno from inside libssl you
  cannot know whether a write hit EPIPE, and a SIGPIPE left pending fires when the mask is restored.
  ~1.1 µs per call. Wrap EVERY call that can write: `SSL_read` (alerts, KeyUpdate answers),
  `SSL_connect` / `SSL_accept`, `SSL_shutdown` and 0-RTT too, not only `SSL_write`.
- Windows and agnos raise no such signal (agnos `sock_send#48` answers -1 for a dead connection).

**TRAPS ON THE WAY.**
1. **The fix changes the SYSCALL SET.** A native TLS socket write is now `sendto` (44 x86_64,
   206 aarch64), macOS adds a `setsockopt`. A seccomp allowlist that admits `write` but not
   `sendto` — kavach's `basic` profile, which kills on a miss — now SIGSYS-kills the writer on its
   first record, and a fallback cannot help because a seccomp kill is not an error return. Record
   a syscall-set change as a ⚠ behaviour change whenever a stdlib leaf switches syscall.
2. **A child-process test passes vacuously under an inherited ignore** — SIG_IGN survives execve.
   The child calls `signal_default(SIGPIPE)` first; a death by signal comes back from `wait_pid`
   as 128 + signo, so 141 is the assertion. And the other way round: **GitHub's runner starts its
   steps with SIGPIPE IGNORED**, so a test that ASSERTS "still SIG_DFL" without setting it reads 1
   on CI only (6.6.14 handoff: `tls_libssl_sigpipe`). Every test sets the disposition it depends on;
   reproduce CI with `perl -e '$SIG{PIPE}="IGNORE"; exec @ARGV' ./test`.
3. **There are two write loops** (plain, and the deadline's non-blocking one): protect the LEAF
   both share and test every row with and without a deadline (gotchas
   `a_guard_inside_one_copier_covers_only_that_copier`).
4. **A forked test peer that writes its records in SEVERAL writes races the side under test**, which
   reads the first, refuses, alerts and closes: a peer descheduled between writes meets the closed
   socket. Before the fix that was a rare SIGPIPE death; after it, a `TLS_ERR_IO` and a wrong child
   exit code — CI's ARM runner hit it, a loaded pi failed 4 of 20 (`tls_native_alert_mapping`) and
   3 of 3 (`tls_native_ccs_deadline`'s write hook). Seal everything the peer sends at that point into
   ONE buffer and write it once.
5. **A reset row that stops at the FIRST failed write is not a RED test on Linux.** Which errno the
   first failing write sees depends on whether the peer's FIN or its RST was processed first: a
   write that finds `ECONNRESET` pending returns it WITHOUT a signal (`sk_stream_error` signals
   only for EPIPE), and only the write after it is EPIPE + SIGPIPE. Keep writing after the first
   failure (net_plain_write_peer_reset.tcyr does three more) so the pre-fix build really dies, and
   accept the SET {EPIPE, ECONNRESET} (Linux 32/104, Darwin 32/54) — never one exact errno, which
   is a flake on loaded hosts.
6. **A CONTROL row keeps a survival test honest:** one child writes the same reset connection with
   a raw `write(2)` and must DIE 141. If the host (or an inherited disposition the child failed to
   reset) would not have killed the writer, the "lives" rows prove nothing — the control turns
   that from a silent pass into a failure on every run.
7. **A spawn-self child does not run under qemu-user** without a binfmt registration: the emulated
   `execve` of an aarch64 ELF fails and the child exits 127 (why `tls_native_socket_transport.tcyr`
   and `regression_wait_unobserved.tcyr` fail under a bare `qemu-aarch64` on the dev box). A test
   that only needs a separate PROCESS, not a fresh image, forks on POSIX — it then runs under qemu
   too — and spawns itself only on Windows. Since 6.7.16 `docs/development/dev-tools-linux.md` says how to run such
   a file under qemu: install `qemu-user-binfmt` (check `ls /proc/sys/fs/binfmt_misc/qemu-aarch64`); `scripts/dev/xb.sh`
   warns when the registration is absent (PL-11; wine_and_qemu_user_are_not_the_platforms_they_emulate_v6716).
8. **A regression must FAIL, not HANG.** A row that writes into a pipe and then reads it back
   blocks for ever when the write regresses to an error (nothing was written, the write end is
   still open). Close the write end before reading: an empty pipe then reads EOF and the row fails
   with a count (found by net-1's ENOTSOCK-fallback mutant; field_notes/compiler/patterns.cyml
   `a_test_that_may_hang_must_fail_not_hang`).

CHANGELOG [6.6.14] CYRIUS-2026-0021; [6.6.16] CYRIUS-2026-0025; [6.7.15] W0-1.

[[entries]]
name = "a_winsock_socket_is_not_a_file_handle"
description = "On Windows `sys_read` / `sys_write` are ReadFile / WriteFile, and those FAIL on a Winsock SOCKET made with WSA_FLAG_OVERLAPPED (lib/net.cyr's sockets), so any stdlib leaf that bottoms out in them cannot carry a socket — native TLS did not run over a net.cyr socket on Windows at all until 6.6.14, and nothing noticed because no cross-OS test ran TLS over a socket. Use ws2_32 recv / send, and keep the file leaf as the fallback for a HANDLE. The rest of the Winsock spellings differ too — and two more surfaced at 6.6.16: an accepted socket inherits a non-blocking listener (on macOS too, never on Linux), and a Win64 int / BOOL result is only eax, so a reroute's rax must be masked"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["platform", "windows", "pe", "winsock", "sockets", "deadline", "testing", "tls", "accept", "nonblocking", "macos", "eax", "v6.6.14", "v6.6.16"]
---
**THE LEAF** (`_tn_os_read` / `_tn_os_write`, `lib/tls_native_conn.cyr`):

    r = syscall(0xF048 /*recv*/ or 0xF047 /*send*/, fd, buf, min(n, 0x7FFFFFFF), 0)
    on failure: e = syscall(0xF024 /*WSAGetLastError*/) & 0xFFFFFFFF
      10038 WSAENOTSOCK, 10093 WSANOTINITIALISED -> sys_read / sys_write (a pipe or file HANDLE)
      anything else -> -e

Measured on cass against the 6.6.13 lib: `tls_native_connect` / `_accept` over a loopback pair
returned `TLS_ERR_IO` at once — 34 of the socket-transport test's 44 assertions failed.

**THE OTHER SPELLINGS.** Mask `WSAGetLastError` to 32 bits — only eax is defined (see A WIN64
`int` RESULT IS ONLY eax below; 6.6.16 masked the remaining sites). Readiness is
`WSAPoll` (POLLRDNORM / POLLWRNORM), sockets only: a HANDLE answers -10038, which must route to
the between-calls fallback, not to an error. Would-block is WSAEWOULDBLOCK 10035, not EAGAIN.
Non-blocking is `ioctlsocket(FIONBIO)`, which CANNOT be read back — so a bounded write leaves
the socket blocking (the mode net.cyr creates it in) where Linux / macOS restore saved F_GETFL
flags.

**AN ACCEPTED SOCKET INHERITS THE LISTENER'S NON-BLOCKING MODE — on Windows AND macOS, not
Linux** (6.6.16). BSD/macOS accept(2) copies the listener's O_NONBLOCK onto the new socket and
Windows accept() copies FIONBIO; Linux accept(2) never does (accept4 SOCK_NONBLOCK is opt-in).
Before 6.6.16 `sock_accept` passed the OS's socket through, so a server with a non-blocking
listener got non-blocking connections on Windows and macOS only — and native TLS, whose
no-deadline read and write need a blocking socket, failed TLS_ERR_IO at once there (an empty recv
under SO_RCVTIMEO returned in 0 ms on cass, ecb, ach). Since 6.6.16 `sock_accept`'s contract is
"the accepted socket is BLOCKING on every target": Windows resets FIONBIO with one ioctlsocket per
accept (it cannot be read back), macOS clears O_NONBLOCK via F_GETFL/F_SETFL only when set, and a
failed reset closes the socket and returns the error. The raw `sys_accept` / `sys_accept4`
wrappers stay OS mirrors and still inherit. A test of "is this fd blocking" that must hold on
Windows cannot read the flag: time an empty recv under a short SO_RCVTIMEO (>= ~250 ms of a
300 ms timeout) instead.

**A WIN64 `int` / BOOL RESULT IS ONLY eax — MASK BEFORE YOU COMPARE IT** (6.6.16). Win64 returns
a C `int`, `BOOL` or `DWORD` in eax and leaves rax[63:32] undefined. A cyrius `syscall(0xF0xx, ...)`
reroute hands back the whole of rax, and only some PE emitters extend it: the 0xF045-0xF04A socket
band is sign-extended (`movsxd`), GetLastError (0xF04B) and the 0xF03D-0xF044 kernel32 BOOLs are
zero-extended (`mov eax, eax`), but WSAGetLastError (0xF024) and the older ws2_32 band
(closesocket, bind, getsockopt, WSAIoctl, connect, WSARecv, WSASend, getaddrinfo, setsockopt,
listen, getpeername, getsockname) are NOT. So in lib code read them as
`syscall(0xF024) & 0xFFFFFFFF`, test `(r & 0xFFFFFFFF) == 0`, or go through `_net_wsa_rc` /
`_net_wsa_err` (net.cyr), `_sw_wsa_err` (syscalls_windows.cyr), `_tn_win_sockerr` (tls_native). An
unmasked `== 0` reads a success as a failure under dirty bits; an unmasked
`0 - syscall(0xF024)` is a huge non-errno; `!= 997` misses WSA_IO_PENDING. The ws2_32 builds
measured on cass happen to zero-extend, so nothing fails today — which is exactly why it is
pinned by a STATIC gate (`tests/gates/platform/pe_wsa_lasterr_masked.sh`) rather than a runtime
row. A `callptr` to a Win64 BOOL function (ConnectEx, AcceptEx) is the same: mask it. The same
class on the C side: CYRIUS-2026-0016 (6.6.13), libssl's `int` returns read zero-extended.

**A TEST TRAP: AFD TAKES THE WHOLE SEND.** A non-blocking `send` of 64 MiB to a peer that never
reads returned 67108864 in ONE call on cass: Windows admits a send whole while the socket's
buffered bytes are under SO_SNDBUF; only the NEXT send sees WSAEWOULDBLOCK. Linux and macOS block
after a few hundred KB. So a "fill the send buffer, expect the deadline" row writes in a loop until
a write does not complete, and asserts the ELAPSED time — once a deadline has passed every later
call answers `TLS_ERR_TIMEOUT` before any I/O, so the result code alone proves nothing.
CHANGELOG [6.6.14] (roadmap backlog j).

[[entries]]
name = "win32_iocp_as_a_per_waiter_wake_slot_pooled_not_per_object"
description = "On PE an I/O completion port is a lib-only wake-up slot for a blocked thread; pool the ports per waiter, never keep them per channel, and create them with a large concurrency value (6.6.16, lib/thread_win.cyr's blocking channel)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["platform", "windows", "pe", "iocp", "threads", "channels", "handles", "v6.6.16"]
---
PE routes no WaitOnAddress, condition variable, event or semaphore. A lib-only blocking wait can
still be built on the IOCP reroutes lib/async_win.cyr already uses:
- PostQueuedCompletionStatus (0xF021) leaves a token in a port;
- GetQueuedCompletionStatus(INFINITE) (0xF020) takes one, and blocks while there are none;
- a token posted before its waiter arrives waits in the port, so a wake-up cannot be lost.

lib/thread_win.cyr's channel (6.6.16) gives each WAITER its own port. The waiter queues a node
{next, port} in its own frame on the channel's receiver or sender FIFO, under the channel lock;
a waker unlinks the oldest node of the other kind under the lock and posts that port one token
after unlocking. The waiter leaves its wait only with its token, and the token is posted only
after the unlink, so a frame never leaves a dangling node behind.

⚠ DO NOT KEEP PORTS PER OBJECT. The first draft kept a receiver port and a sender port in each
channel, created lazily and never closed (channels are never freed). A consumer that makes a
reply channel per request then leaks a kernel handle per request: measured on cass, 400 such
channels took a process from 84 handles to 484. Ports come from a process-wide pool instead: a
call takes one when it first has to wait and gives it back when it returns, so handles scale with
the threads waiting at once (the same 400 channels: 85). The pool lives in static storage, never
the heap, because alloc_reset would pull it out from under a waiter.

⚠ A port goes back to the pool, or is closed, only when no token can still reach it. A closed
handle's VALUE can be reused by the next port created, and a token in flight would then wake that
port's waiter with its node still queued. If GetQueuedCompletionStatus fails without a token,
unlink the node if no waker claimed it; if one did, abandon the port rather than close it.

⚠ CreateIoCompletionPort's 4th argument is the port's CONCURRENCY. 0 means "one running thread per
CPU", and the port then RELEASES NO FURTHER WAITER while that many threads it released are still
running, even with tokens queued. That is right for an I/O reactor and wrong for a wake-up slot.
Pass 0x7FFFFFFF. User-facing surface: language/features.cyml `chan_blocking_capability_v6616`.

[[entries]]
name = "dce_compaction_moves_a_kernels_data_v6711"
description = "Since cyrius 6.7.11 a `kernel;` image (or a CYRIUS_WX=0 executable) built with CYRIUS_DCE=1 is SMALLER and its `.bss` / `.rodata` sit LOWER than in the plain build; a linker script, boot shim or loader that hard-codes section addresses must read them from the image. A `--pie` kernel and aarch64 still NOP-fill (same size as the plain build)."
category = "cyrius"
---
Through 6.7.10 `CYRIUS_DCE=1` only NOP-filled a kernel image (6.6.20 – 6.7.10; 6.5.72 – 6.6.19 compacted it and broke
every data address). Since 6.7.11 the dead fns are REMOVED and the data — which follows the code in a kernel image
and in a `CYRIUS_WX=0` executable — moves down with it, by a multiple of 16, every data address in the code moving
with it. So the section addresses of a DCE build are not the plain build's: read them from the image
(`readelf -SW`), never from a previous build. Under W^X (a normal executable) the data segment stays where it was.
Declined (NOP-fill, same size, named in the note): a `--pie` kernel, `shared;`, aarch64, Mach-O, PE.
A dead fn the compiler could not size (an `in` / `out` in `asm`) stays in the image as a kept dead body
(diagnostics_caps.cyml a_kept_dead_fn_is_ud2_and_the_syms_map_predates_compaction_v6711). The boot gate
(`dce_kernel_compaction_boots.sh`) boots a compacted multiboot1 image under QEMU. CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (INF-5).

[[entries]]
name = "inline_asm_mnemonics_match_whole_and_out_writes_its_width_v6714"
description = "Since cycc 6.7.14 an `asm { }` mnemonic is matched on its WHOLE name: a misspelling (`clix`, `inb`, `outb`, `nopx`) no longer assembles as its prefix but is refused by name; `asm { in al, dx; }` compiles; and `out dx, eax` / `out dx, ax` write 32 / 16 bits (they wrote one byte, EE). A kernel that relied on the byte write says `out dx, al`."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "asm", "inline asm", "in", "out", "port I/O", "mnemonic", "kernel", "agnos", "aarch64", "cx", "D16"]
---
    asm { in al, dx; }        # 6.7.14: EC — through 6.7.13 "unexpected in" (`in` lexes as the for-in keyword)
    asm { out dx, eax; }      # 6.7.14: EF, a 32-bit write — through 6.7.13 EE, i.e. `out dx, al` (one byte)
    asm { clix; }             # 6.7.14: error: unknown x86 asm mnemonic 'clix' — through 6.7.13 it assembled `cli`
    asm { in bl, dx; }        # 6.7.14: error: in takes al, ax or eax and then dx, not 'bl'

WHOLE NAMES (D16, IN-14). Both assemblers matched a mnemonic by its first bytes, so a misspelling compiled as the
instruction its name starts with: `clix` was cli, `inb al, dx` in, `outb dx, al` out, `stix` sti, `mfencex` mfence, and
`param_loadx(..)` compiled; on aarch64 `nopx`, `dmbx`, `retx`, `eretx`. Each is refused by name since 6.7.14 (`unknown x86
asm mnemonic 'clix'`, `unknown aarch64 asm mnemonic 'nopx'`). Every spelling the guide, lib/ and the corpus use keeps its
bytes.

`in` / `out` TAKE THE ACCUMULATOR AND `dx`, AND THE ACCUMULATOR'S WIDTH IS THE ENCODING (IN-13): `in al|ax|eax, dx` is
EC / 66 ED / ED, `out dx, al|ax|eax` EE / 66 EF / EF. Through 6.7.13 `in` was reachable only through a misspelling
(`inb`), and both skipped their operands, so `out dx, ax` / `out dx, eax` wrote ONE byte. ⚠ A kernel that wrote `out dx,
eax` and relied on the byte write now writes 32 bits — say `out dx, al`. Any other operand is refused by name; an
immediate port is raw bytes (`asm { 0xE4; 0x60; }` = `in al, 0x60`). aarch64 has no `in` / `out` (refused by name); cx
has no inline asm (`inline asm not supported in cyrius-x: 'cli'`).

LEFT OPEN IN 6.7.14, CLOSED IN 6.7.15: `lgdt` / `lidt` / `invlpg` / `mov crN` / `ltr` / `int N` ignored their operand
register (`lgdt [rbx]` assembled `lgdt [rax]`, `int 256` was `int 0`), and the guide's `#ifplat aarch64` example
`asm { dmb ish }` did not compile (aarch64 mnemonics took no operand) —
inline_asm_operands_name_their_register_and_aarch64_barriers_take_options_v6715. A dead fn holding an `in` / `out` is
kept whole under `CYRIUS_DCE=1` (dce_compaction_moves_a_kernels_data_v6711). language/core.cyml (the inline asm block).
CHANGELOG [6.7.14] IN-13, IN-14.

[[entries]]
name = "inline_asm_operands_name_their_register_and_aarch64_barriers_take_options_v6715"
description = "Since cycc 6.7.15 x86 `lgdt` / `lidt` / `invlpg [reg]`, `mov crN, reg` / `mov reg, crN` and `ltr reg16` encode the register they NAME — through 6.7.14 each used rax / ax, and `mov cr8, rax` wrote CR0 — and refuse by name what they cannot take (a crN other than 0 / 2 / 3 / 4 / 8, a 32-bit register, `ltr eax`, `int 256`). On aarch64 a bare `dsb` is DSB SY (it was SSBB, which orders no memory access) and `wfe` is WFE (an MSR that faulted SIGILL at EL0), and `dmb` / `dsb` take an option (`dmb ish`, D37). qemu-user ran the wrong words without a trap. Since 6.7.16 `param_load` reads its register by the whole 64-bit name too (`r8d` loaded r8, `x31` was x3: refused, 6.7.16's D33)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.15", "v6.7.16", "asm", "inline asm", "kernel", "agnos", "lgdt", "lidt", "invlpg", "cr3", "cr8", "ltr", "int", "param_load", "aarch64", "dmb", "dsb", "isb", "wfe", "barrier", "qemu", "D16", "D37", "D33"]
---
    asm { mov cr3, rbx; }      # 6.7.15: 0F 22 DB, CR3 <- rbx     — through 6.7.14: CR3 <- rax (the operand was skipped)
    asm { mov cr8, rax; }      # 6.7.15: 44 0F 22 C0, writes CR8  — through 6.7.14: 0F 22 C0, wrote CR0
    asm { lgdt [eax]; }        # 6.7.15: error: lgdt takes one 64-bit register in brackets, [rax] to [r15], not 'eax'
    asm { dmb ish; }           # aarch64, 6.7.15: D5033BBF        — through 6.7.14: unknown aarch64 asm mnemonic 'ish'

x86 KERNEL OPERANDS (IT-6; 6.7.14's D16 for the refusals). Each emitter stepped over its operand unread and encoded rax
/ ax whatever the source named: `lgdt [rbx]` was `lgdt [rax]`, `lidt [rcx]` / `invlpg [rdi]` likewise, `mov cr3, rbx`
was `mov cr3, rax`, `mov rbx, cr3` was `mov rax, cr3`, `ltr bx` was `ltr ax`; `mov cr8, rax` WROTE CR0, `mov cr9, rax`
was `mov cr1, rax`, and `int` kept the vector's low 8 bits (`int 256` was `int 0`). Since 6.7.15 each uses the register
it names, r8 – r15 included. ⚠ Kernel code that staged the value in rax but NAMED another register must name rax now.
Refused by name: a control register other than cr0 / cr2 / cr3 / cr4 / cr8; a register that is not a whole 64-bit name
beside crN or in the brackets (`mov cr3, eax`, `lgdt [eax]`, `lgdt [raxx]`); an `ltr` operand that is not a 16-bit
register (`ltr eax`, `ltr r8d`); an `int` vector over 255. Every spelling the guide, lib/ and the corpus use is the one
the emitters hard-coded, so nothing shipped changed.

aarch64 BARRIERS (IT-7, IT-8). The bare words were the wrong instructions: `dsb` was SSBB (a speculative-store-bypass
barrier — it orders NO memory access), `wfe` an MSR that faults SIGILL at EL0, and `dmb` / `isb` the reserved #0 option
(run as sy). Since 6.7.15 bare `dmb` / `dsb` / `isb` are the SY barriers and `wfe` is WFE — code that relied on `dsb`
for the completion of device or page-table writes (a kernel's TLB maintenance) gets that barrier for the first time. A
barrier also takes its option (6.7.15's D37): `dmb` / `dsb` with sy st ld ish ishst ishld nsh nshst nshld osh oshst
oshld, and `isb sy`; anything else is refused naming it ("dmb takes sy, st, ld, ish, … not 'foo'"). The guide's
`#ifplat aarch64` example `asm { dmb ish }` compiles at last. `svc #0` cannot be written (the `#` starts a comment):
write `svc`.
⚠ qemu-user ran every one of the wrong words without a trap — a passing run under qemu proves nothing about a barrier's
encoding: decode it (llvm-objdump) or run it on hardware. `param_load(raxx, 0)` still compiled as rax in 6.7.15 — closed
in 6.7.16 (below).

6.7.16 — `param_load` TAKES THE WHOLE 64-BIT NAME (6.7.16's D33, TL-8). `param_load(reg, n)` matched its register by a
prefix, so a longer spelling loaded the register it starts with: `raxx` was rax, and `param_load(r8d, 0)` — never a
32-bit load — loaded all of r8 (`r8w` / `r10d` likewise); on aarch64 `x31` .. `x39` were x3, `x0abc` / `x1y` x0 / x1.
Each is refused naming it since 6.7.16; write the 64-bit name (`r8`). `eax` / `w0` were refused before and keep that,
now in the same words. language/core.cyml (the inline asm block). CHANGELOG [6.7.15] IT-6, IT-7, IT-8; [6.7.16] TL-8.

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_header_return_types_and_agnos_hosts_v6711"
description = "`cyrius header` refuses a return it cannot size from one file (another file's struct, a generic instance, a struct holding one, `#ifdef`'d fields, three values) with rc 1, the other prototypes still printed; on Win64 a 9–16 B / `dyn` / two-value return is a comment, not a prototype; since 6.7.12 a `: Result` / `: Option` / `: Tagged` pair is modelled and an unannotated pair or a vector return refused; since 6.7.14 a wrapped parameter list is read whole. Build agnos images on an x86_64 Linux host: every non-x86_64-Linux CLI refuses `--agnos` and the x86 bare-metal target by name (the aarch64 Linux CLI since 6.7.12). Build PE images on x86_64 Linux or Windows: since 6.7.14 every other CLI refuses `--win` by name (a Mac wrote a Mach-O named `.exe`)."
category = "cyrius"
---
CYRIUS HEADER (6.7.11). A public fn's return becomes `cyr_val` (8 B or less, a pointer, a scalar, `Str`), `cyr_val2` (a
9–16 B struct, a `dyn`, a two-value return under the x86_64 SysV guard — aarch64 returns two values in x0:x2, not
AAPCS64's x0:x1) or `cyr_valN` (over 16 B, the rounded size: the callee writes whole words). A public fn returning
another file's struct, a generic instance, a struct holding one, a struct with `#ifdef`'d fields or three values makes
`cyrius header` exit 1 (the other prototypes still printed): make the fn private / `_`-named or wrap it. On Win64 a
9–16 B / `dyn` / two-value return gets a named comment, not a prototype (Win64 C returns anything over 8 B through a
hidden pointer, so cycc's rax:rdx pair matches no Win64 C type). SINCE 6.7.12: a `: Result` / `: Option` /
`: Tagged` (or the file's own `enum E: stack`) return is `cyr_val2` (tag in w0, payload in w1) under the x86_64 SysV
guard, a named comment on aarch64 (the pair is x0:x2 there) and Win64; a public fn whose signature says a word but whose
body returns the pair (`fn f(x): i64 { return Ok(x); }`) is refused by name, rc 1 (6.7.12's D23); a SIMD vector return
is refused by name, rc 1 (6.7.12's D24). SINCE 6.7.13 a vector is never a `cyr_val`, as a return OR as a parameter
(TL-4: `'<f>' takes '<param>: <type>': a SIMD vector parameter, which no C11 type carries`), and a `?` on a pair call
makes the fn a pair (TL-5) — declare such a fn `: Result` / `: Option` / `: Tagged`, take `*V` instead of `V`, or make
it private. lib/result.cyr's `ok_via` / `err_via` are `: Result` now, so `cyrius header lib/result.cyr` is rc 0 (D37);
since 6.7.14 lib/net.cyr's `sock_send_a` / `sock_recv_a` are declared `: Result` too, so `cyrius header lib/net.cyr`
prints both as `cyr_val2` (6.7.14's TL-4).
A PUBLIC SIGNATURE MAY WRAP (6.7.14, TL-3). Through 6.7.13 a wrapped parameter list was read from its FIRST LINE
only: the generated prototype was short, and a C caller that compiled against it passed garbage in the dropped
arguments. Since 6.7.14 the list is read to its own `)`: every parameter is printed, a vector parameter on a
continuation line is refused by name (rc 1), and a list of blanks alone (`fn e1( )`) prints `(void)`. REGENERATE a
header made from a file with wrapped lists — a C caller of the short prototype now meets "too few arguments". The
documented limit stands: a pair reached only through another file's fn or a method is not seen — declare such a
wrapper `: Result`.

AGNOS / BARE-METAL IMAGES BUILD ON AN x86_64 LINUX HOST. `CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` / `CYRIUS_ELF64_KERNEL` are read by
`src/main.cyr` only. The Windows and macOS CLIs refuse `--agnos`, `--target=x86_64-bare-metal-elf` and
`--target=aarch64-bare-metal-elf` by name, rc 1, before compiling; since 6.7.12 (D6) the aarch64 Linux CLI refuses
`--agnos` and `--target=x86_64-bare-metal-elf` by name too (through 6.7.11 it wrote an aarch64 Linux binary at rc 0), and
any two emit targets in one run are refused (D20). Since 6.7.13 `--win` there is refused by name too, and `--aarch64` /
aarch64 bare metal run the host's own cycc (TL-2). On a macOS host `--win` wrote a Mach-O named `.exe` at rc 0 and
printed `OK` through 6.7.13; since 6.7.14 both macOS CLIs refuse it by name, rc 1, nothing written (6.7.14's TL-1):
`error: --win is not available from the macOS CLI: cycc emits Mach-O only (build PE images on x86_64 Linux or
Windows)`. The rule: PE images are built on x86_64 Linux or on Windows only — run `file` on any `.exe` a Mac build
produced before 6.7.14. (The aarch64 Linux CLI's `[deps.X] target` axis: platform_abi.cyml
the_aarch64_linux_install_has_the_toolchain_but_typescript_v6714.) CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (TL-10, TL-11), [6.7.12] (TL-10,
TL-22, TL-23, TL-30), [6.7.13] (TL-2, TL-4, TL-5, TL-16), [6.7.14] (TL-1, TL-3, TL-4).

[[entries]]
name = "open_a_directory_you_will_list_with_o_directory_v6711"
description = "Open a directory you will `getdents` with O_DIRECTORY: `open(path, O_RDONLY)` on a FIFO BLOCKS until a writer appears, before any ENOTDIR could be reported, so one FIFO planted where a server lists a directory freezes it. With O_DIRECTORY the kernel refuses a non-directory at path resolution (Linux, and Darwin, which checks it before the FIFO open). lib/fs.cyr's listers do since 6.7.11."
category = "cyrius"
---
    var fd = sys_open(&cpath, O_RDONLY | O_DIRECTORY | O_CLOEXEC, 0);   # -ENOTDIR (-20) on a FIFO or a file

`open(path, O_RDONLY)` on a FIFO waits for a writer BEFORE the caller can learn it is not a directory: one FIFO
planted where a server lists a directory freezes it (agora 1.9.1's poll server). With `O_DIRECTORY` the kernel refuses
a non-directory at path resolution — Linux does, and so does Darwin, which checks `O_DIRECTORY` before the FIFO open
(measured on ecb and ach; macOS now answers -20 for a regular file too, not -22). A `stat`-first guard leaves a
stat / open swap open; `O_DIRECTORY` closes it. `lib/fs.cyr`'s listers (`dir_list`, `dir_list_checked`,
`dir_list_into`) open this way since 6.7.11 (language/stdlib_modules.cyml dir_list_contract_v6711), and
`lib/audit_walk.cyr`'s `_aw_dir_list_typed` since 6.7.12 (two instances now). agnos already required `AO_DIRECTORY`.
THE SECOND HALF OF THE LESSON (6.7.12): hand the kernel exactly the Str's bytes (`_fs_cpath`), never `str_data(path)` —
a Str is not NUL-terminated at its end, and an embedded NUL would open a PREFIX; `_fs_cpath` refuses both (4096+ bytes, an
embedded NUL: -36). CHANGELOG [6.7.11], [6.7.12] LIB-4.

[[entries]]
name = "tagged_handles_exact_ranges_and_time_bounds_where_zero_means_none_v6711"
description = "Three agnos lessons from 6.7.11, each general: recognise a tagged handle range EXACTLY (`fd >= TAG && fd <= TAG + 7`), never by a bit test on a signed value (`fd & TAG` took -1 for slot 7); a time bound where 0 means no bound must never receive a clamped-to-zero time left; a capability probe that can also fail for the call's own reason caches no only after a fallback proves the call was good."
category = "cyrius"
---
RECOGNISE A TAGGED HANDLE RANGE EXACTLY. Write `fd >= TAG && fd <= TAG + 7`, never `fd & TAG`: every negative i64 has
every high bit set, so `fd & TAG` took a failed open's -1 for slot `-1 & 7 = 7`. On agnos `sys_write(-1)` wrote to the
8th socket's peer and `sys_close(-1)` tore its connection down, returning 0 (fixed 6.7.11). THE CLASS: a table indexed by
`fd & 7` must first test that `fd` IS a tagged socket fd. 6.7.11: `_agnos_is_sock_fd` exact, `_agnos_fd_conn`,
`sys_close`. 6.7.12 (LIB-2): the four slot helpers (`_agnos_sock_bind`, `_agnos_listen_stash`, `_agnos_listen_start`,
`_agnos_fd_listen`) answer -1, and the `lib/net.cyr` arms answer `Err(9)` / `Err(88)` — Linux's EBADF / ENOTSOCK — before
any table write or kernel call; `sock_connect` / `net_connect_nb` test BEFORE `#47`, or the conn opened for a bad fd
leaks.

A TIME BOUND WHERE 0 MEANS "NO BOUND" MUST NEVER RECEIVE A CLAMPED-TO-ZERO "TIME LEFT". `sock_send_dl`#109's
`timeout_us` is one such bound; Linux reads a negative `SO_SNDTIMEO` the same way. Test the deadline first and break,
or pass a negative "no wait" — `max(0, left)` turns "time's up" into "wait forever".

A "DOES THE KERNEL HAVE #N" PROBE THAT CAN ALSO FAIL FOR THE CALL'S OWN REASON caches "no" only after a fallback proves
the call was good. #109 answers -1 both on a pre-1.57.13 kernel and for a dead or foreign conn; caching "no #109" on a
dead conn would cost every later write its deadline on a kernel that has #109. `_agnos_sock_send_left` caches it only
when the `#48` fallback then answers >= 0. Compiler side: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
agnos_is_an_x86_host_that_is_not_linux_and_the_predefines_do_not_say_so (4b). CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (the stdlib lane).

[[entries]]
name = "sys_sigprocmask_works_on_macos_per_thread_v6712"
description = "On macOS `sys_sigprocmask` works since 6.7.12 and is PER-THREAD, as on Linux (Darwin's `__pthread_sigmask`, 329, via the 1329 alias). Code that took its old -78 as 'no signal mask on macOS, skip' now really blocks or unblocks; an exec-family child starts with an empty mask there too."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "macos", "darwin", "signals", "sigprocmask", "per-thread", "exec", "process.cyr"]
---
- On macOS `sys_sigprocmask` works since 6.7.12 and changes the CALLING THREAD's mask, as on Linux (Darwin's
  `__pthread_sigmask`, 329). Code that took its old -78 as "no signal mask on macOS, skip" now really blocks or
  unblocks.
- Darwin's sigset is 32 bits: the wrapper takes the usual 8-byte set and zeroes `oldset`'s upper word, so the same
  8-byte buffers work on every target.
- Do NOT issue Darwin's own `sigprocmask` (48) for a thread's mask: XNU applies it to EVERY thread of the process (a
  two-thread probe on ach and ecb: the other thread's mask read 536870912 after the main thread blocked SIGUSR1).
- An exec-family child on macOS now starts with an EMPTY mask (Linux since 6.7.9). A child that must keep a signal
  blocked blocks it itself.
language/features.cyml sys_sigprocmask_on_macos_v6712. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] DW-5.

[[entries]]
name = "xflock_on_windows_waits_and_answers_minus_11_v6712"
description = "`xflock` on Windows (6.7.12) is a real lock that leaves the data readable and writable through other handles; `LOCK_NB` on a held lock answers -11 (no `EAGAIN` constant on PE — compare with -11); a blocking lock now WAITS where it returned -1 at once; the lock is per handle; since 6.7.13 a re-lock to the held mode keeps the lock."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "windows", "pe", "xflock", "flock", "LockFileEx", "EAGAIN", "patra", "wine"]
---
`xflock` on Windows is a real lock since 6.7.12 (kernel32 `LockFileEx` on one sentinel byte far past the data), and it
leaves the file's DATA readable and writable through every other handle — it excludes other lockers only, as flock
does. What changes for code that ran on Windows before:

- `LOCK_NB` on a held lock answers -11. There is no `EAGAIN` constant on PE (lib/syscalls_windows.cyr declares none),
  so compare with -11 (Darwin's EAGAIN is -35).
- A blocking `LOCK_EX` / `LOCK_SH` now WAITS where it returned -1 at once — a program that treated -1 as "Windows has
  no locks, go on" now blocks behind a real holder.
- The lock is per HANDLE: a second `file_open` of the same file is another locker.
- -22 for an op that is not SH / EX / UN; `LOCK_UN` of a lock not held is 0.

6.7.13 — A RE-LOCK KEEPS THE LOCK (LIB-1). Under 6.7.12, re-taking the mode a handle already held DROPPED the lock
first: a handle waiting for it could get in (patra lost a second handle's rows inside a transaction), and a `LOCK_NB`
re-lock could fail -11 and leave nothing held. Since 6.7.13 it is flock's no-op — re-lock freely (patra re-takes
`LOCK_EX` per statement). A mode CHANGE (SH ↔ EX) is still not atomic, on every target: a waiter may get in, and
`LOCK_NB` may then fail -11 with the handle holding NO lock — check the result. On Windows a held lock occupies bytes
2^63 − 3 … 2^63 − 1 of the file (never data); a program's own `LockFileEx` on those bytes would collide with `xflock`.

A Windows test of lock exclusion needs REAL Windows: wine does not enforce a byte lock against another handle's I/O,
and wine shows a lock hand-off race only sometimes. language/stdlib_modules.cyml file_locking. CHANGELOG [6.7.12]
WN-1, [6.7.13] LIB-1.

[[entries]]
name = "getpwuid_zero_on_macos_is_root_since_6_7_12"
description = "Before 6.7.12 `pwd_getpwuid(0)` / `grp_getgrgid(0)` on macOS answered `nobody` (Darwin's first record is uid / gid -2, read as 0) and `pwd_getpwuid(-2)` found nothing. Since 6.7.12 they answer root / wheel and -2 finds nobody; a passwd / group line with a malformed id is skipped on every target."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "macos", "darwin", "pwd", "grp", "passwd", "nobody", "uid", "gid", "wheel"]
---
Darwin ships `nobody:*:-2:-2:` (and `nogroup:*:-1:`) AHEAD of root. The readers stopped at the first non-digit and
never checked the field's end, so on macOS `pwd_getpwuid(0)` / `grp_getgrgid(0)` answered `nobody`, and
`pwd_getpwuid(-2)` found nothing. Since 6.7.12 a uid / gid field is ONE decimal number with an optional leading `-`:
uid 0 is root, gid 0 is `wheel`, -2 finds nobody, and a line whose id field is not one number is skipped on every
target (`grp_getgrouplist` adds no gid for it).
⚠ Darwin's gid 0 is `wheel`, not `root` — a cross-platform test that compares the group name of gid 0 must accept both.
language/stdlib_modules.cyml identity_lookups_musl_pattern. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] WN-2r.

[[entries]]
name = "windows_native_tls_trusts_what_the_chain_engine_trusts_minus_fetching_v6712"
description = "The Windows native trust store since 6.7.12: a NotBefore-dated root anchors only leaves issued before its date (D9); any certificate the disallowed CTL or the Disallowed store names fails the chain at ANY depth; a root Windows would fetch on demand is not there, so a server under it fails TLS_ERR_CERT_INVALID until the program adds the root. TLS_CTX_LEN is 632. A CTL entry is a crypt32 property, not a certificate hash: since 6.7.14 the 48-byte signature-hash entries are applied too, and since 6.7.15 the 20 / 32 / 64-byte ones — each certificate judged by the entries of its own signature digest's length; since 6.7.16 a 16-byte entry under both readings (a public-key MD5, and an MD2 / MD5-signed certificate's signature hash)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "v6.7.14", "v6.7.15", "v6.7.16", "windows", "tls", "tls_native", "trust store", "crypt32", "NotBefore", "disallowed", "CTL", "TLS_CTX_LEN", "SHA-1", "SHA-256", "SHA-384", "SHA-512", "MD2", "MD5", "prop 15", "D57"]
---
- A NotBefore-dated root (the root program's issuance-date distrust) anchors only leaves issued BEFORE its date, as
  SChannel does (D9) — through 6.7.11 such a root was refused whole.
- Any certificate the auto-updated disallowed CTL or the Disallowed store names fails the chain at ANY depth, not only
  as the root. A present but unreadable CTL makes `tls_native_set_ca_system` fail (`TLS_ERR_IO`).
- The disallowed CTL lists NO certificate hashes: an entry is one of two crypt32 properties. Prop 25 is the MD5 of the
  subjectPublicKey's key bytes (16 B). Prop 15, the "signature hash", is the tbsCertificate hashed with the
  certificate's OWN signature digest, so its length follows that digest — 20 B for SHA-1, 32 for SHA-256, 48 for
  SHA-384 — and a SHA-384-signed certificate's entry matches no hash of its DER. The 2025 list's 48-byte entries are
  the six DarkMatter intermediates (confirmed on cass: each `CERT_TRUST_IS_EXPLICIT_DISTRUST` with the Disallowed stores
  empty). Through 6.7.13 the client applied prop-25 entries only; since 6.7.14 it applies the 48-byte prop-15 entries
  too (6.7.14's D57; SHA-384-signed certificates, one extra SHA-384 each, Windows only). A prop-15 entry of another
  length (20 / 32 / 64 B) was not applied in 6.7.14; since 6.7.15 it is (PL-14). An entry's LENGTH names the
  certificate's own signature digest — 20 SHA-1, 32 SHA-256, 48 SHA-384, 64 SHA-512 — and a certificate is judged only
  by the entries of its digest's length. Latent: no certificate a system-roots ctx meets today is listed at those
  lengths. A 16-byte entry is ambiguous by length — a public-key MD5 (prop 25) or an MD2 / MD5-signed certificate's
  signature hash (prop 15); 6.7.15 read it as the first only. Since 6.7.16 (PL-10) it is applied under BOTH readings, so
  an MD2 / MD5-signed certificate is judged by the 16-byte entries — unconfirmed (no read-only probe shows whether the
  chain engine compares it with prop 15) and latent; only RSASSA-PSS and Ed25519 certificates stay unjudged by prop 15.
  A trust policy holding only 16-byte deny keys now parses each certificate's signatureAlgorithm, and an unreadable one
  is distrusted (fail-closed).
- A root Windows would fetch on demand is NOT there (cyrius does not fetch — D9), so a server under it fails
  `TLS_ERR_CERT_INVALID` until the program adds the root (`tls_ctx_load_verify_locations`). A Windows box shows only
  the roots fetched so far (40 on cass), so a floor sized by a Linux bundle (> 50 anchors) is a Linux fixture.
- A program that hard-codes the ctx size reads `TLS_CTX_LEN` (632 since 6.7.12; 624 before).
language/stdlib_modules.cyml tls_module_contract_v6614 (TRUST STORE). CHANGELOG [6.7.12] WN-6, [6.7.14] PL-10,
[6.7.15] PL-14, [6.7.16] PL-10.

[[entries]]
name = "windows_symlink_long_paths_v6712"
description = "`xsymlink` on Windows (Developer Mode or elevated) takes link paths and absolute targets of any length since 6.7.12; a relative target is stored as written with `/` turned into `\\` (an NT link does not read `/` as a separator); an absolute target of 248+ units is passed `\\\\?\\`-prefixed. A raw `syscall(0xF03E, …)` takes UTF-8 since 6.7.12."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "windows", "pe", "xsymlink", "CreateSymbolicLinkW", "MAX_PATH", "long paths", "UTF-8"]
---
- `xsymlink` on Windows (it still needs Developer Mode or an elevated process) takes a link path of ANY length (it
  failed -1 past 260 units and was refused at 519+) and an ABSOLUTE target of any length (one whose full form is 248+
  units is passed `\\?\`-prefixed).
- A RELATIVE target is stored as written, with `/` turned into `\`: an NT link stores its target verbatim and resolves
  it later against the link, and it does not read `/` as a separator (a `subdir/inner.txt` target never resolved). It
  is never made absolute — that would change what the link means when the tree moves.
- A raw `syscall(0xF03E, …)` takes UTF-8 since 6.7.12 (UTF-16 before) — call `xsymlink` instead.
language/stdlib_modules.cyml file_locking (xsymlink). CHANGELOG [6.7.12] WN-4, WN-4r.

[[entries]]
name = "x86_64_epoll_event_is_packed_and_the_stride_is_the_layout_v6712"
description = "x86_64's `struct epoll_event` is EPOLL_PACKED — 12 bytes, data @4 — while aarch64's is 16 bytes, data @8, and the STRIDE is part of the layout: v6.4.42 fixed the data OFFSET per arch, but lib/async.cyr kept a 16-byte stride on every arch until 6.7.12. Walk an epoll_wait array by EPOLL_EVENT_SIZE through the readers."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "epoll", "epoll_event", "packed", "x86_64", "aarch64", "stride", "async", "EPOLL_EVENT_SIZE"]
---
- x86_64 is `EPOLL_PACKED`: 12 bytes, data @4. aarch64 is unpacked: 16 bytes, data @8.
- v6.4.42 (field_notes/compiler/retros/resolved_traps.cyml, its BUG 2) fixed the data OFFSET per arch — but
  `lib/async.cyr` kept a 16-byte STRIDE on every arch. So on x86_64 the 2nd..8th event of one `epoll_wait` were read 4,
  8, … bytes off. Level-triggered epoll re-reported the missed fd on the next wait, so nothing hung and every test
  passed.
- Never stride an `epoll_wait` array by 16 on x86_64: use `EPOLL_EVENT_SIZE` / `epoll_event_data` (6.7.12). A struct's
  layout includes its SIZE, not just its field offsets.
- WRITE a stack epoll_event with `epoll_event_set(ev, events, data)` (6.7.14, LIB-4), never a hand-gated `+4` / `+8`:
  it puts the events at 0 and the data at the peer's offset (@4 on x86_64 Linux and x86 macOS, @8 on aarch64) and
  returns `ev` for `sys_epoll_ctl`; `ev` is a buffer the caller owns (`var ev[16]`, at least `EPOLL_EVENT_SIZE`).
  `epoll_event_new` is `alloc(16)` plus the writer — one 16-byte leak per registration on the bump allocator.
  `lib/async.cyr` has no `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` offset pair left. Do not define your own `epoll_event_set`: the
  last definition wins for the stdlib's callers too (semantics_runtime.cyml
  last_definition_wins_reaches_the_stdlibs_own_callers_v6714).
- Methodology: a switch called "value-identical" in a plan was identical on one arch only. A row that holds two events
  of ONE wait (two ready pipes, one `_async_poll` waking both waiters) is what caught it.
language/stdlib_modules.cyml epoll_event_readers_v6712. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] LIB-5, [6.7.14] LIB-4.

[[entries]]
name = "linux_arg_max_is_not_a_constant_2_mib_v6712"
description = "Linux ARG_MAX is RLIMIT_STACK / 4, capped at 6 MiB, and an unprivileged `ulimit -s` raises it — read /proc/self/cmdline to EOF, never with one read of a fixed buffer. `args_init` did that until 6.7.12, and `argc()` silently described a 2,097,152-byte prefix. Return a negative errno on failure, never 0 (macOS's success value). Since 6.7.16 the compilers read their own command line whole too, and an environment is forwarded through `env_block`, never /proc (macOS has none)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "v6.7.16", "linux", "macos", "ARG_MAX", "argv", "args_init", "proc", "cmdline", "environ", "env_block", "exec_cmd", "RLIMIT_STACK", "secureexec"]
---
- Linux ARG_MAX is RLIMIT_STACK / 4, capped at 6 MiB (3/4 of the kernel's 8 MiB `_STK_LIM`), and an UNPRIVILEGED
  `ulimit -s` raises it — it is not the constant 2 MiB that `getconf ARG_MAX` prints under the default limit.
- Read `/proc/self/cmdline` to EOF, never with one read of a fixed buffer. `args_init` did that until 6.7.12, and
  `argc()` silently described a 2,097,152-byte prefix. It now doubles a 2 MiB first buffer until EOF and publishes
  nothing on failure.
- A secureexec (setuid / file caps) clamps the soft stack limit to 8 MiB, so a privileged exec chain carries at most
  2 MiB of argv + envp — why the truncation was a bug, not a vulnerability.
- Return a NEGATIVE errno on failure, never 0, because 0 is macOS's success value (D7). Test `args_init() < 0`.
- THE COMPILERS' OWN COMMAND LINE (6.7.16, TL-9). cycc, the aarch64 compilers, the Linux arm of cycc_win and cycc_cx
  read /proc/self/cmdline with ONE 255-byte read through 6.7.15: a `--lex-ts` / `--parse-ts` / `--emit-js` path ending
  past byte 255 was cut (the cut name opened), and a flag past it dropped silently (`cycc <300-byte arg> --version`
  compiled its empty stdin, rc 0). Since 6.7.16 they read the whole line, and 2 MiB or more stops them by name
  (diagnostics_caps.cyml live_compiler_capacity_ceilings). The macOS drivers and cycc.exe always read it whole.
- /proc IS LINUX'S (6.7.16, TL-11, TL-11r). `/proc/self/environ` does not exist on macOS — a read of it is a silent
  empty environment. Forward your environment with lib/io.cyr's `env_block(&len)` (getenv's block on Linux and macOS,
  0 on Windows and agnos), never /proc. `exec_cmd`'s child gets the caller's whole environment on macOS since 6.7.16 (it
  got none) and on Linux past 64 KB (it was cut); a program that relied on the empty one passes an exact one through
  `exec_env`.
language/stdlib_modules.cyml args_init_contract_v6712. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] LIB-14; [6.7.16] TL-9, TL-11, TL-11r.

[[entries]]
name = "cx_pointer_wrappers_outside_cxvms_translated_set_v6712"
description = "On the cx target a pointer-taking syscall outside cxvm's translated set cannot work. Since 6.7.14 (D55) cxvm answers every guest syscall it does not translate, serve or list as scalar-only with -38, raw `syscall()` included, without reaching the host; `mmap_stack` answers -1 and `fl_alloc` 0. Since 6.7.14 `alloc()` is the guest break cxvm serves, so a `.cyx` that allocates and its cxvm come from ONE release. Since 6.7.15 the listed calls run the call they name on every host (they were renumbered on aarch64 and macOS hosts), and mkdir / rmdir / unlink / rename / symlink / readlink / stat act on the host. Since 6.7.16 a macOS host answers with Linux's errno and signal numbers, F_GETFL is x86_64 Linux's word, and getdents64 / mknodat / wait4 / truncate / `poll(NULL, 0, ms)` run on every POSIX host."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "v6.7.14", "v6.7.15", "v6.7.16", "cx", "cxvm", "guest contract", "mmap", "pipe", "fstat", "unlink", "stat", "mkdir", "rename", "getdents64", "mknodat", "wait4", "truncate", "kill", "F_GETFL", "sleep_ms", "sandbox", "ENOSYS", "brk", "alloc", "errno", "macos", "aarch64", "windows", "D55", "D28", "D29"]
---
- cxvm translates `read` / `write` / `open` (and `lseek` / `close` / `exit` at their own arity) and serves `getrandom`
  / `clock_gettime` (through the host's stdlib) and, since 6.7.14, `brk` (in guest space).
- Since 6.7.12 four wrappers refuse honestly (D13): `cyr_mmap` -1 (so `mmap_anon` 0), and `sys_pipe` / `sys_fstat` /
  `sys_unlink` -38 (`sys_unlink` acts on the host since 6.7.15, below). Since 6.7.14 the two raw-`mmap` paths that
  still returned a HOST address as success do too: `mmap_stack` (lib/thread.cyr) answers -1 and `fl_alloc`
  (lib/freelist.cyr's `_fl_mmap`) 0 — it trapped cxvm on first use (LIB-5).
- 6.7.14 — EVERY OTHER NUMBER ANSWERS -38 (6.7.14's D55, LIB-6). Through 6.7.13 everything else reached the HOST kernel
  with the guest's raw registers, so a guest pointer arrived as a host address — and since the 8 MiB guest it could be
  cxvm's own image on Linux (text 0x400000, data 0x600000: `cyr_munmap(0x400000, 65536)` unmapped cxvm, exit 139; an
  fstat at 0x600100 wrote over cxvm's globals). Now only the translated and served calls and a SCALAR-ONLY list
  (exit_group, fork, dup / dup2, getpid / getppid, kill, sched_yield, fsync / fdatasync, flock, ftruncate, fchmod /
  fchown, the uid / gid getters, socket, listen, unshare, timerfd_create, epoll_create1, and fcntl's scalar commands)
  reach the host; every other number — stat, statfs, munmap, mprotect, mmap, nanosleep, mkdir (no directory is made),
  uname, wait4, futex, the address-taking socket calls, ioctl, `fcntl(F_GETLK)`, a raw `syscall()` — answers -38
  without reaching it (stat and mkdir are served since 6.7.15, below). ⚠ A pointer call that worked by accident — a
  null pointer (`wait4(pid, 0, …)`, `accept(fd, 0, 0)`), `getrlimit` — answers -38 too: check every syscall result on
  cx. cxvm is still not a sandbox for the calls it
  passes. In 6.7.14, on aarch64 Linux and macOS hosts, a passed call could be renumbered into ANOTHER host call (a guest
  lseek ran getxattr on pi) — fixed in 6.7.15, below.
- 6.7.15 — THE LISTED CALLS RUN THE CALL THEY NAME ON EVERY HOST, AND THE PATH CALLS ACT ON THE HOST (PL-11, PL-12).
  cxvm passed the listed calls to the host as a RUNTIME number, which the aarch64 and Mach-O compilers renumbered as if
  it were their own: on aarch64 Linux a guest `lseek` ran getxattr, `kill` ran lseek and `fork` ran close (the parent
  took the child branch); on arm64 macOS `getppid` / `dup` / `lseek` / `sched_yield` / `exit_group` killed cxvm with
  SIGSYS; on both Macs `open`'s Linux flag bits read as other Darwin flags. Each now runs through the host peer's
  wrapper. And `mkdir`, `rmdir`, `unlink`, `rename`, `symlink`, `readlink` and `stat` act on the host (they answered
  -38; through 6.7.14 `sys_unlink` removed nothing, so a probe file a cx program made STAYED): `xmkdir`, `xstat`,
  `file_rename` and `file_write_atomic` work on cx, and a program that tested for that -38 sees the host's answer (-2
  for a missing name). What still differs by host (as of 6.7.16):
    - off x86_64 Linux and Windows, `open`'s flag bits past the access mode and the eight named flags are dropped;
      `F_GETFL` reports those nine plus x86_64 Linux's O_LARGEFILE since 6.7.16 (below) — and an `open()`ed FIFO's
      F_GETFL on a Mac has no O_LARGEFILE;
    - off x86_64 Linux a cx `stat` fills only the Stat enum's seven fields (mode, nlink, uid, gid, size, mtime and its
      nsec): st_dev / st_ino / st_blocks / atime / ctime read 0;
    - on a Darwin host a getdents64 count under 64 is -22 (Darwin's floor), and one under 1048 may end a /dev listing
      early with -22 (getdirentries64_has_no_buffer_too_small_answer_v6716);
    - on a Windows host the calls whose PE peer is a stub (getppid, kill, dup, dup2, flock, fchmod, fchown, the uid /
      gid getters, sched_yield, fork, fcntl) answer -38, and so do readlink, stat, getdents64, mknodat and wait4.
    In 6.7.15 a macOS host also answered a failed call with Darwin's errno (ELOOP reached the guest as -62, not -40) and
    passed `kill`'s signal number to Darwin unmapped (a guest SIGUSR1 was Darwin's SIGBUS) — both closed in 6.7.16.
- 6.7.16 — THE GUEST SEES x86_64 LINUX ON EVERY HOST, AND FOUR MORE CALLS RUN (PL-4 – PL-8, PP-4; 6.7.16's D28, D29):
    - on a macOS host a failed call answers x86_64 Linux's errno (PL-4), and `kill` sends the signal the guest names
      (PL-5) — a guest testing Linux numbers reads them there too;
    - `fcntl(fd, F_GETFL)` answers x86_64 Linux's word on every host, O_LARGEFILE (0x8000) included for an opened file
      or device, never a pipe or socket (D29, PL-6): code that compared the whole word (`== O_RDWR`) instead of masking
      the access mode now differs on aarch64 Linux and macOS hosts, as it already did on x86_64 Linux;
    - getdents64, mknodat, wait4 and `poll(NULL, 0, ms)` run on every POSIX host (they answered -38), so `xgetdents`,
      `sys_mknodat`, `sys_waitpid` and wait4 work under cxvm, and `sleep_ms` sleeps (it returned at once); a poll with
      pollfds is still -38 everywhere; on a Darwin host wait4's status word and WCONTINUED are Linux's (PL-7, PL-7r);
    - `sys_truncate` acts on the host file (it answered -38; PP-4);
    - on a Windows host `open(path, O_CREAT | O_EXCL, …)` over an existing directory answers -17 (it was -13), so
      `file_create_exclusive` / `file_write_atomic` past a directory planted at a temp name behave as on every host;
      the crossos rows a Windows host has no route for print `not run` by name (D28, tests only; PL-8).
- 6.7.14 — THE HEAP IS THE GUEST BREAK, SO A `.cyx` AND ITS cxvm COME FROM ONE RELEASE (LIB-7). cx `alloc()` bumps over
  a break cxvm serves in guest space — the free guest memory between the image and 1 MiB below the stack, ~7 MiB less
  the image; memory it grants reads zero. The 128 KB static heap is gone from every allocating `.cyx` (an `alloc(16)`
  program: 151,546 → 22,494 B), and exhaustion now reads `alloc: cx heap exhausted at <N> B (the guest break cannot grow
  by <M> B more)` (it read `alloc: cx static heap exhausted (128 KB)`). ⚠ A 6.7.14 `.cyx` that allocates stops on a
  6.7.13 cxvm at its first allocation with `cxvm: guest address out of range: <a large number>` — the host's program
  break, which the old cxvm handed back. Run a `.cyx` on its own release's cxvm. An older `.cyx` (static heap) runs on
  the new cxvm.
language/features.cyml cx_tail_calls_and_cxvm_guest_traps_v6612 (6.7.12, 6.7.14). CHANGELOG [6.7.12] LIB-7, LIB-8;
[6.7.14] LIB-5, LIB-6, LIB-7; [6.7.15] PL-11, PL-12; [6.7.16] PL-4 – PL-8, PL-7r, PP-4.

[[entries]]
name = "pie_on_macos_is_arm64_only_v6713"
description = "Since cycc 6.7.13 `cyrius build --pie` on an Intel Mac is refused by name (the x86_64 Mach-O is fixed-address); on Apple Silicon it is accepted and changes nothing (every arm64 Mach-O is already MH_PIE); neither macOS compiler reads CYRIUS_PIE, so an exported CYRIUS_PIE=1 is silently inert on both."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.13", "macos", "darwin", "mach-o", "pie", "MH_PIE", "--pie", "CYRIUS_PIE", "D43"]
---
    cyrius build --pie app.cyr app     # Intel Mac:      error: --pie is not available from the macOS x86_64 CLI:
                                       #                 its cycc writes a fixed-address Mach-O (no MH_PIE mode)
    cyrius build --pie app.cyr app     # Apple Silicon:  accepted — the output is MH_PIE either way (flags 0x00200084)
    CYRIUS_PIE=1 cyrius build app.cyr  # either Mac:     inert — neither Mach-O compiler reads it

PIE is a Linux ELF mode (x86_64 RIP-relative, aarch64 adrp / add), plus arm64 macOS by construction. Through 6.7.12
`--pie` on an Intel Mac wrote a plain fixed-address Mach-O at rc 0 — the flag silently did nothing. The Windows CLI
refuses `--pie` by name since 6.7.12 (cycc.exe has no PIE mode). language/tooling.cyml cyrius_cli, compiler_flags.
CHANGELOG [6.7.13] TL-3 (D43).

[[entries]]
name = "windows_file_calls_answer_a_negative_errno_v6714"
description = "Since 6.7.14 a failed open / read / write / seek on Windows answers a negative errno through GetLastError (-2 not found, -13 access denied, -17 exists, -9 a bad handle, -32 a closed pipe, -28 a full disk, -22 a bad seek) — through 6.7.13 every failed open answered -1 and every failed read / write / seek 0. Test a file call's result with `< 0`, never `== -1` or `== 0`. A directory is -13 to a plain open. Since 6.7.15 a close answers 0 or a negative errno too (it handed back CloseHandle's BOOL, 1 / 0), a seek on fd 0 / 1 / 2 reaches the std stream (it failed -9), and `sys_dup2` answers -38. Since 6.7.16 the rest answer Linux's errno too — mkdir / unlink / rmdir / truncate / ftruncate, rename / symlink / fsync — a std fd closes, a seek on a pipe or console is -29, a directory's unlink / truncate / write-open is -21, and a negative or low-bit fd is -9."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "v6.7.15", "v6.7.16", "windows", "pe", "errno", "GetLastError", "ReadFile", "WriteFile", "SetFilePointerEx", "SetEndOfFile", "SetStdHandle", "GetFileType", "CreateFileW", "CloseHandle", "MoveFileExW", "CreateSymbolicLinkW", "FlushFileBuffers", "file_open", "file_close", "file_close_r", "file_rename", "xlseek", "xmkdir", "xunlink", "xrmdir", "xsymlink", "xfsync", "sys_ftruncate", "sys_truncate", "sys_dup2", "ESPIPE", "EISDIR", "IoNotFound", "IoBadFd", "wine", "D55", "D30", "D31", "D78", "D79", "D80", "D94", "D95"]
---
    var fd = file_open("missing.txt", O_RDONLY, 0);
    if (fd < 0) { … }                  # -2 since 6.7.14 (-1 before) — never `fd == -1`
    var n = file_read(fd, buf, 64);
    if (n < 0) { … }                   # -9 for a bad handle (0 before) — 0 is end of file, not a failure

Through 6.7.13 every failed `CreateFileW` answered -1, and every failed ReadFile / WriteFile / SetFilePointerEx answered
0: ReadFile and WriteFile zero the byte count BEFORE they fail, and SetFilePointerEx leaves the new position untouched,
so the reroute's count never told end of file — or a seek to 0 — from a failure. Since 6.7.14 the BOOL is checked and a
failure answers a negative errno through `GetLastError` (one shared table): -2 not found / path not found, -13 access
denied or a sharing violation, -17 exists, -36 a name too long, -9 a bad handle, -32 a closed pipe, -28 a full disk,
-22 a bad seek; -1 only for a Win32 code with no POSIX peer (and a path the UTF-16 widening refuses). So:
  - Test a file call's result with `< 0`, never `== -1` (an errno is not -1) and never `== 0` for a failure.
  - `file_open_r` / `file_read_all_r` say `IoNotFound` for a missing file (they said IoOther).
  - A pipe whose writer closed still reads 0: ERROR_BROKEN_PIPE (and ERROR_HANDLE_EOF) IS end of file; ERROR_MORE_DATA
    is a delivered read and keeps its count.
  - A DIRECTORY is ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED to `CreateFileW`: a plain open of one (no `O_DIRECTORY`; an `O_NOFOLLOW`
    read-only open too) is -13, and so was `O_CREAT|O_EXCL` over an existing directory from `file_open` until 6.7.16
    (-17 since — below); `file_create_exclusive` still says -17 on every target (its Windows arm asks the name). Wine
    answers ERROR_FILE_EXISTS for the second, so only real Windows shows it. `O_CREAT|O_EXCL` over an existing file or
    link is -17 (it was -1).
  - A seek on fd 0 / 1 / 2 failed -9 in 6.7.14 (0 before it): the seek passed the std fds as raw HANDLE values, where
    the other reroutes map them through `GetStdHandle`. Since 6.7.15 it reaches the std stream (6.7.15's D55, PL-8):
    with a redirected stdin, `xlseek(0, n, SEEK_SET)` moves where the next read starts. In 6.7.15 a seek on a PIPED or
    CONSOLE std stream SUCCEEDED on real Windows without moving the stream (wine answered -1). Since 6.7.16 a seek on a
    pipe, a socket or a console answers -29 (`ESPIPE`) on Windows as on Linux and macOS (6.7.16's D31, PL-3), so
    `xlseek(0, 0, SEEK_CUR) < 0` tells a piped stdin there too; a disk file and NUL seek as before.
  - A CLOSE answers 0 or a negative errno since 6.7.15 (PL-7). `sys_close` / `file_close` handed back CloseHandle's BOOL
    — 1 for a good close, 0 for a failed one — so every `< 0` test on a Windows close was dead. A handle that is not
    open is -9 now, and `file_close_r` is `Ok(0)` / `Err(IoBadFd)` (it was `Ok(1)` / `Ok(0)`). Test a close with
    `< 0` / `== 0`, never `!= 0` as success. In 6.7.15 `sys_close(0 / 1 / 2)` answered -9 there and left the std stream
    open. Since 6.7.16 it closes the std stream and answers 0, as on Linux (6.7.16's D30, PL-2): a later write / read /
    seek on the fd and a second close answer -9, a HANDLE another std slot holds (a `run_capture` child's one pipe as
    stdout and stderr) stays open for that slot, and `sys_ftruncate(0 / 1 / 2, n)` of a redirected std stream truncates
    its file (it answered -1). A close of a NEGATIVE fd answers -9 since 6.7.16 (6.7.16's D78, PP-1): -1 and -2 are the
    current-process / current-thread pseudo-handles, which CloseHandle "closed" with TRUE, so a failed open's -1 closed
    unchecked read as a good close (`file_close_r(-1)` is `Err(IoBadFd)`, it was `Ok(0)`).
  - `sys_dup2` answers -38 for every call since 6.7.15 — it answered `newfd` and redirected nothing (LF-5;
    agnos_two_sided_abi_mirror).
6.7.16 — THE REST OF THE FILE CALLS ANSWER LINUX'S ERRNO (6.7.16's D79, D80, D94, D95; PP-2, PP-2r, PW-3, PW-4). Each
answered -1 for every failure through 6.7.15 — test `< 0`, NEVER `== -1`:
  - `sys_mkdir` / `sys_unlink` / `sys_rmdir` / `xmkdir` / `xunlink` / `xrmdir`: an existing name -17, a non-empty
    directory -39, a missing path -2, access denied -13 (D79; a path that is not valid UTF-8 is still -1);
  - `file_rename` / `sys_rename` of a missing source or into a missing directory -2; `xsymlink` / `sys_symlink` under a
    missing directory -2, over an existing name -17, elevated or not; `xfsync` / `sys_fsync` / `sys_fdatasync` of a bad
    fd -9, of a READ-ONLY fd -13 — Linux and macOS fsync a read-only fd (0) (D94). A link Windows policy refuses (no
    Developer Mode, not elevated) is the one -1 left, and a path error is answered before that check;
  - `sys_ftruncate` of a descriptor it cannot truncate — a pipe, a console, a device (`NUL`), a read-only one, and
    (Windows only: SetEndOfFile needs FILE_WRITE_DATA, which an O_APPEND handle lacks) an O_APPEND one — is -22, a bad
    fd -9 (D80); `sys_truncate` answers its open's errno, and `sys_truncate("NUL", n)` -22. (macOS truncates its
    /dev/null by fd as a no-op, 0, and refuses it by path, -22.);
  - "IS A DIRECTORY" HAS THREE SPELLINGS ACROSS TARGETS (D95): Linux answers -21 to an unlink, truncate or write-open of
    a directory; macOS -21 to the open and truncate but -1 (EPERM) to the unlink; Windows -21 to all three since 6.7.16
    (-13 before). A read-only plain open of a directory is still -13 on Windows (Linux opens it), and an exclusive
    create over a directory is -17 everywhere (`O_CREAT|O_EXCL` from `file_open` answered -13 on real Windows). Ask the
    NAME (`xstat` / `is_dir`) rather than reading a failed unlink's errno.
  - The raw reroutes `syscall(0xF03F, h)` (SetEndOfFile) and `syscall(0xF03E, ..)` (CreateSymbolicLinkW) answer 0 /
    -errno, not the BOOL: a raw caller's `!= 0` success test inverts.
  - An fd above 2 with a low bit set answers -9 in every fd reroute and in the socket, lock and final-path wrappers
    (PW-1, PW-2): an_fd_is_never_derived_by_arithmetic_v6716.
The measured before / after table is the guide's File I/O paragraph. language/core.cyml stdlib (io.cyr);
language/stdlib_modules.cyml file_locking. CHANGELOG [6.7.14] PL-1, PL-2, PL-3; [6.7.15] PL-7, PL-8, LF-5; [6.7.16]
PL-2, PL-3, PP-1, PP-2, PP-2r, PW-1 – PW-4.

[[entries]]
name = "an_aarch64_wx_executable_is_at_least_64_kb_v6714"
description = "An aarch64 W^X executable is at least ~64 KB (`syscall(60, 7);`: 65,888 B; 520 B with CYRIUS_WX=0; 4,448 B on x86) and its size moves in 64 KB steps, so a 6 KB code change can add 64 KB to the file. Read code growth from the code PT_LOAD's FileSiz (`readelf -l -W <bin> | grep LOAD`), never from the file size. Kept by design (6.7.13's D45); documented and pinned in 6.7.14."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "aarch64", "elf", "W^X", "binary size", "PT_LOAD", "p_align", "CYRIUS_WX", "readelf", "D45"]
---
An aarch64 W^X executable puts its data at a 64 KB-aligned FILE offset (0x10000 for a small program), so it carries 0 –
64 KB of zero padding outside both LOADs' FileSiz and grows in 64 KB steps: `syscall(60, 7);` is 65,888 B (520 B with
`CYRIUS_WX=0`, 4,448 B on x86), and a 6 KB code change can add 64 KB to the file (550 live fns, ~90 KB of code, put the
data at 0x20000). That is the price of exact W^X on 4, 16 and 64 KB-page arm64 kernels: the loader maps whole pages, and
only a 64 KB-aligned data offset keeps the code's last page and the data's first apart on all three (kept by the user,
6.7.13's D45). x86's 4 KB p_align makes its split nearly free. So measure code growth from the code `PT_LOAD`'s
`FileSiz` (`readelf -l -W <bin> | grep LOAD`), never from the file size. `CYRIUS_WX=0` removes the padding — and W^X with
it (one RWE segment): not a fix to ship. CHANGELOG [6.7.14] IN-15.

[[entries]]
name = "the_aarch64_linux_install_has_the_toolchain_but_typescript_v6714"
description = "Since 6.7.14 the aarch64-linux release tarball ships every `[release]` bin built for aarch64 — the `cyrius` CLI, cyrfmt, cyrlint, cyrdoc, cyrius-lsp and the rest (it shipped only cycc, cyrsign, cyrius-init and cyriusly) — except cybs, and `ts_test_runner` fails every `.ts` file there (the aarch64 compiler has no TS front end). And a plain `cyrius deps` on aarch64 Linux resolves `[deps.X] target = \"aarch64\"` for its own arch (it vendored the x86_64 dep). Since 6.7.15 (D38) the aarch64 compiler carries the TypeScript front end too — the entry's name records the 6.7.14 gap. Since 6.7.16 the macOS and Windows tarballs ship every bin too (the macOS `bin/cyriusly` is a compiled Mach-O; Windows' `cyriusly.exe` refuses its POSIX verbs by name, D84), and arm64 macOS's `--target=js` writes the JavaScript (D50)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "v6.7.15", "v6.7.16", "aarch64", "linux", "pi", "macos", "windows", "release tarball", "install", "cyrius CLI", "cyriusly", "cyrius-lsp", "TypeScript", "--parse-ts", "--emit-js", "--target=js", "deps", "target", "D59", "D38", "D50", "D84"]
---
- THE TARBALL (6.7.14's D59, PL-9). Through 6.7.13 the aarch64-linux tarball shipped only cycc, cyrsign, cyrius-init and
  cyriusly: an aarch64 binary install had no `cyrius build`, `test` or `repl`. Since 6.7.14 it ships every `[release]
  bins` entry built for aarch64 and arch-checked, and the `[release] scripts` (cyrius-watch.sh among them) as every POSIX
  tarball does; `bin/cyriusly` is the compiled version manager there too. cybs is the one bin not shipped: it is x86_64
  seed-assembled, with no aarch64 form.
- TYPESCRIPT (6.7.15's D38, PL-13). Through 6.7.14 the aarch64 compiler had no TS front end, and the aarch64 drivers
  skipped any flag they did not know: `--parse-ts` compiled stdin as cyrius and exited 0 on valid cyrius, so
  `ts_test_runner` failed every `.ts` file on aarch64. Since 6.7.15 every compiler but `cycc_cx` carries the front end:
  `--lex-ts` / `--parse-ts` / `--emit-js` run TypeScript on aarch64 Linux, macOS and Windows (exit 0 / 1, JS on
  stdout), and `cyrius build --target=js` works on aarch64 Linux and macOS (on Windows it is still refused by name, now
  pointing at `cycc.exe --emit-js`). A script that relied on the flag being ignored sees a TS verdict now; the compilers
  there are ~0.26 – 0.34 MB larger.
- THE DEPS TARGET AXIS (TL-2). Through 6.7.13 a plain resolve on the aarch64 Linux CLI vendored the x86_64 dep (a
  `[deps.X] target = "x86_64"` / `"x86"` entry), at rc 0. Since 6.7.14 it vendors `target = "aarch64"` / `"arm64"` deps
  and skips `"x86_64"` / `"x86"` ones, as `--aarch64` does. Re-run `cyrius deps` there after the pin bump.
- THE macOS AND WINDOWS TARBALLS (6.7.16, TL-11, TT-11 – TT-14; 6.7.14's D59 applied to them, D84). Since 6.7.16 they
  ship every `[release] bins` entry too: macOS adds cyrius-lsp (it published nothing on macOS: it read /proc), ark,
  cyrld, ts_test_runner, cyrsign-efi and the COMPILED cyriusly — `sh bin/cyriusly` fails now: run it directly, or use
  the shell twin at versions/<v>/scripts/cyriusly. Windows adds ark, cyrld, ts_test_runner, cyrsign-efi and
  `cyriusly.exe`, whose install / update / setup / uninstall / cmdtools / `use --global` refuse by name (D84; use
  install.ps1) and whose home without `CYRIUS_HOME` is `%USERPROFILE%\.cyrius` (with HOME set it was `$HOME/.cyrius`);
  `cyrius sign-efi` on Windows runs the `cyrsign-efi.exe` beside it (it always failed, rc 127).
- `--target=js` ON arm64 macOS (6.7.16's D50, TT-15). 6.7.15's "`cyrius build --target=js` works on aarch64 Linux and
  macOS" was false on arm64 macOS: with no selector the CLI refused it ("runs this host's cycc, not the compiler another
  target selector chose"), since 6.7.12's D20. Since 6.7.16 it writes the JavaScript there too.
language/tooling.cyml (the release / install text; cyrius_cli). CHANGELOG [6.7.14] PL-9, TL-2; [6.7.15] PL-13; [6.7.16]
TL-11, TT-11 – TT-15.

[[entries]]
name = "an_fd_is_never_derived_by_arithmetic_v6716"
description = "On Windows an fd above 2 IS the HANDLE, and the NT kernel ignores a handle value's two low bits: through 6.7.15 `h + 1` … `h + 3` beside an open `h` WAS `h` — `sys_close(h + 3)` closed h and answered 0, a write to `h + 1` went into h's file, `sock_close(s + 3)` closed the socket s. Since 6.7.16 such an fd answers -9 everywhere, as on Linux. Linux has its own alias (an fd is an `unsigned int`: `h + 2^32` IS h). Never derive an fd by arithmetic, and a test that probes 'an fd never opened' asks first."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.16", "windows", "pe", "linux", "fd", "HANDLE", "alias", "close", "EBADF", "sockets", "closesocket", "xflock", "tests", "harness", "F_GETFD"]
---
    var h = file_open("a.txt", O_WRONLY, 0);
    sys_close(h + 3);              # 6.7.16: -9 on every target — through 6.7.15 on Windows: 0, and h was closed
    sys_write(h + 1, "x", 1);      # 6.7.16: -9 — through 6.7.15 on Windows: the byte went into a.txt

THE MECHANISM (PW-1, PW-2). On Windows a cyrius fd above 2 is the HANDLE value itself, and handles are multiples of
four: the kernel, Winsock and kernel32's lock and final-path calls resolve a value ignoring its two low bits. So through
6.7.15 a computed or stale fd one to three past a live one reached CloseHandle / ReadFile / WriteFile /
SetFilePointerEx / FlushFileBuffers / SetEndOfFile, `closesocket`, `listen`, `getsockname`, `LockFileEx` as the live one
— `sys_close(h + 3)` answered 0 and closed h (whose own write and close then failed -9), a write to `h + 1` landed in
h's file, an ftruncate of `h + 2` truncated it, `xflock(h + 1, LOCK_EX)` locked h's file. Since 6.7.16 an fd above 2
with a low bit set answers -9 before any Win32 call — close, read, write, seek, fsync / fdatasync, ftruncate, the socket
verbs (`sock_*`, `sys_getsockname` / `sys_getpeername` / `sys_listen`, `fd_wait_ready`; `sock_send` / `sock_recv` give
`Err(9)`), `xflock`, the native TLS transport and the async reactor's socket tasks; `win_handle_under` answers -1. No
descriptor cyrius returns has a low bit set. It is the class of 6.7.16's D78 (a negative fd reached CloseHandle as a
pseudo-handle: windows_file_calls_answer_a_negative_errno_v6714) in another shape.

LINUX HAS ITS OWN ALIAS: an fd is an `unsigned int`, so `h + 2^32` IS h there (a write succeeds, the close answers 0).
THE RULE: never derive an fd by arithmetic — keep the value the call returned.

THE TEST TRAP (PW-1r). A harness can hand the process extra descriptors (`./t 5>file`), so a row that writes to,
truncates or closes `h + k` WITHOUT ASKING destroys the harness's file. Ask first — `fcntl(fd, F_GETFD)` answers -9 for
a closed fd — and skip, by name, any fd the process inherited.
CHANGELOG [6.7.16] PW-1, PW-2, PW-1r.

[[entries]]
name = "a_macos_decline_answers_darwins_enosys_minus_78_v6716"
description = "Darwin numbers its errnos differently from Linux: ENOSYS is 78 there, and 38 — Linux's ENOSYS — is Darwin's ENOTSOCK. Every macOS stdlib decline answers -78 since 6.7.16 (6.7.16's D76: `sys_prctl`, `sys_gettid` and `sys_sched_kicks` answered -38, 'not a socket' to a caller testing Darwin's numbers). Test `< 0`, or sigil's `ENOSYS` (78 on macOS). And a decline can start working: `sys_mknodat` makes a FIFO on both Macs since 6.7.16 (it answered -78)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.16", "macos", "darwin", "errno", "ENOSYS", "ENOTSOCK", "decline", "sys_prctl", "sys_gettid", "sys_sched_kicks", "mknodat", "mknod", "FIFO", "mkfifo", "D76"]
---
    var r = sys_gettid();          # macOS 6.7.16: -78 (Darwin's ENOSYS) — 6.7.15: -38, which is Darwin's ENOTSOCK
    if (r < 0) { … }               # test < 0 — never a Linux errno number on a Mac
    sys_mknodat(AT_FDCWD, "/tmp/f", S_IFIFO | 0600, 0);    # macOS 6.7.16: 0, a FIFO — 6.7.15: -78

DARWIN'S NUMBERS ARE NOT LINUX'S (PP-5). The stdlib's convention on macOS is Darwin's numbering — the Mach-O syscall
path hands back a negated BSD errno, and every macOS decline answers -78 (ENOSYS there). Through 6.7.15 `sys_prctl` and
`sys_gettid` declined with -38 and `sys_sched_kicks` (no macOS arm) fell into its -38: Linux's ENOSYS, and to any caller
testing Darwin's numbers "not a socket". Since 6.7.16 all three answer -78, and `macos_declines_answer_darwin_enosys.sh`
holds every macOS arm to it. Test a decline with `< 0`, or compare with sigil's `ENOSYS` (78 on macOS), never a literal
38. (A cx guest is the opposite case: cxvm hands it x86_64 Linux's numbers on a macOS host since 6.7.16 —
cx_pointer_wrappers_outside_cxvms_translated_set_v6712.)

A DECLINE THAT STARTS WORKING (PL-7). `sys_mknodat(AT_FDCWD, path, S_IFIFO | mode, 0)` — or an absolute path under any
dirfd — makes a FIFO on both Macs since 6.7.16, over Darwin's `mknod` (it answered -78). `mknod` takes no dirfd, so a
relative path under another dirfd still answers -78, as does any node type but a FIFO (Linux makes a regular file
through mknodat; Darwin refuses one unprivileged). Code that fell back to `/usr/bin/mkfifo` on -78 keeps working. The
shape of sys_sigprocmask_works_on_macos_per_thread_v6712. CHANGELOG [6.7.16] PP-5, PL-7.

[[entries]]
name = "getdirentries64_has_no_buffer_too_small_answer_v6716"
description = "Darwin's getdirentries64 has no reliable 'buffer too small' answer: macOS 13 spins forever, unkillable, on a buffer no record fits (its directory stays locked); macOS 27 refuses under 64 bytes (EINVAL) and answers 0 — a false end of directory — when the next record does not fit. Never hand it under 1048 bytes. Since 6.7.16 (D77) `xgetdents` does not, and answers the records that fit, then -22, as Linux does: treat its -22 as 'grow the buffer'."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.16", "macos", "darwin", "getdirentries64", "getdents64", "xgetdents", "directory", "devfs", "fdesc", "EINVAL", "hang", "cx", "cxvm", "D77"]
---
    var n = xgetdents(fd, buf, 4096);   # 6.7.16, every target: > 0 bytes read, 0 the true end, -22 "grow the buffer"

THE TRAP (PP-6). Linux's getdents64 answers -22 when the next record does not fit. Darwin's getdirentries64 does not:
  - on macOS 13 a buffer no record fits never returns — the process spins in the kernel, unkillable, and its directory
    stays locked;
  - on macOS 27 a buffer under 64 bytes is refused (EINVAL), and one the next record does not fit answers 0 — a false
    end of directory that loses that entry and every one after it.
Through 6.7.15 `xgetdents` handed Darwin the caller's size as it was.

THE FIX (6.7.16's D77). Never hand getdirentries64 under 1048 bytes (`sizeof(struct direntry)`): read into a buffer of
at least that, put the directory back through its lseek cookie, and re-read exactly the records that fit the caller's
buffer. `xgetdents` does all of this since 6.7.16: under 1048 bytes it answers the records that fit, then -22; `n` under
64 is -22 — treat -22 as "grow the buffer". ⚠ devfs and fdesc (no extended readdir) convert from old dirents read into
3/8 of the buffer, in their own offsets, and can return fewer records, or none, on that re-read: a /dev listing under
1048 bytes may END EARLY with -22 (ecb's /dev with 96 bytes after 591 of 634 entries), never with an entry repeated. A
cx guest's getdents64 on a Darwin host's cxvm does the same since 6.7.16 (PL-7r; before the review fix it repeated
entries or looped on /dev). CHANGELOG [6.7.16] PP-6, PL-7, PL-7r.

[[entries]]
name = "wine_and_qemu_user_are_not_the_platforms_they_emulate_v6716"
description = "An emulator is another platform. Under wine a drive-relative path like `/etc/ssl/cert.pem` opens the Linux host's own file, its crypt32 lacks the root-program certificate properties and a fresh prefix's ROOT store is empty; qemu-user has no process_vm_readv / writev (-38) and runs a self-spawning test only with a binfmt registration. Detect the emulator and name what it lacks; reproduce on real hardware (cass, pi), and never relax a row on real Windows."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.16", "wine", "qemu", "qemu-user", "qemu-aarch64", "binfmt_misc", "emulator", "tests", "windows", "aarch64", "crypt32", "trust store", "process_vm_readv", "wine_get_version", "D75"]
---
WINE IS NOT WINDOWS (PP-14):
  - a drive-relative path like `/etc/ssl/cert.pem` opens the Linux host's OWN file, not `C:\etc\...` in the prefix — so
    the planting class never shows under wine (a_rooted_posix_path_is_drive_relative_on_windows_and_any_local_user_can_
    plant_it);
  - its crypt32 answers ERROR_CALL_NOT_IMPLEMENTED for the root-program certificate properties — 84 (name constraints),
    104 / 122 (disable date and scope), 126 / 127 (NotBefore and scope) — and a fresh prefix's ROOT store is empty;
  - the error codes differ: `NUL`'s SetEndOfFile is ERROR_INVALID_HANDLE under wine and ERROR_INVALID_FUNCTION on real
    Windows; wine lets an O_APPEND handle truncate (real Windows refuses); an exclusive create over a directory and a
    seek on a pipe answered other codes (windows_file_calls_answer_a_negative_errno_v6714); a byte lock is not enforced
    against another handle's I/O (xflock_on_windows_waits_and_answers_minus_11_v6712); NTFS's zero-fill never shows
    (a_write_far_past_eof_is_a_huge_real_write_on_ntfs_v6715).
  Detect wine through ntdll's `wine_get_version` and name what it lacks — a row it cannot run prints `not run` by name
  (6.7.16's D75) — and never relax that row on real Windows (cass).

QEMU-USER IS NOT A KERNEL:
  - it has no process_vm_readv / process_vm_writev: they answer -38 under qemu-aarch64. On aarch64 a -38 is also a
    mis-routed syscall number's signature (raw_syscall_numbers_on_aarch64_and_the_darwin_at_flag_divergence), so nothing
    is excused there: reproduce such a row on pi;
  - a self-spawning test (one that execs argv(0)) fails, exit 127, without a binfmt_misc registration. Since 6.7.16
    `docs/development/dev-tools-linux.md` says how — install `qemu-user-binfmt`, check `ls
    /proc/sys/fs/binfmt_misc/qemu-aarch64` — and `scripts/dev/xb.sh` warns when it is absent (PL-11);
  - it runs a wrong instruction word without a trap: a passing barrier under qemu proves nothing about its encoding
    (inline_asm_operands_name_their_register_and_aarch64_barriers_take_options_v6715).
Real hardware is the test — cass for Windows, pi for aarch64 (CLAUDE.md's cross-OS rule); a launcher can hide a bug on
hardware too (shell_runtime.cyml a_parent_with_a_std_fd_closed_hands_that_number_to_its_next_fd_v6715: `env` on pi).
CHANGELOG [6.7.16] PP-14, PL-11.
